{"id": "b375c14c-389b-47d5-a4fa-f4bb9557dc5e", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:53:10.361230", "source": "user", "parent_id": "e5c4c4ab-f262-42a6-ac4b-142649482a3e", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"This is a design-only task: produce a final experiment design (no execution) that answers whether TorchOpt's explicit gradient differentiation API, which reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation, will reduce computation time and improve numerical stability versus standard PyTorch autograd implementations. For this initial inspection pass, please do NOT draft the full design yet. Specifically, I need from you: (1) A short repository/source inspection — note that the workspace is bubblewrap with disabled network and no preinstalled dependencies; inspect whatever is available under /workspace (e.g., tree listing, any TorchOpt docs or source snippets present, README fragments). Report what you actually saw, with paths and brief excerpts. If nothing relevant exists locally, say so explicitly. (2) A proposed approach outline that I will review before you start drafting: candidate workload(s) (e.g., a small MLP, a CNN, a meta-learning / bilevel loop if TorchOpt emphasizes implicit differentiation), candidate loss families (convex regression vs non-convex deep net) since the numerical-stability claim depends strongly on conditioning, the TorchOpt API surface you intend to invoke (e.g., differentiable_optim, MetaOptimizer, functorch-style APIs), the proposed control(s) (vanilla torch.autograd, torch.autograd with create_graph, torch.func, hand-rolled implicit-differentiation baseline), the two response variables (wall-clock per step / total solve time AND a stability metric such as gradient-norm condition number, residual error vs an analytical Jacobian, or loss-curve variance) and how each will be operationalized, and how you will hold hardware/dtype/blas/threading constant across runs. (3) A list of consequential assumptions and uncertainties I should weigh before approving: expected direction and magnitude of the time/memory effect, sensitivity to problem class, whether the API truly avoids retraversing the inner graph, fairness of the autograd baselines (e.g., retain_graph, double-back), and any setup detail not in the supplied topics that you consider load-bearing. Stop after delivering items (1)-(3); do not produce the full experimental write-up yet.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"Will the explicit gradient differentiation API in TorchOpt, which reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation, reduce computation time and improve numerical stability compared to standard autograd implementations?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
