apped around the inner function, where momentum/Adam buffers are reused across the K inner steps.\\n- **B. Differentiable functional optim + implicit gradient** — `torchopt.diff.implicit` / `MetaImplicitGradient` style API that reuses fixed-point / Neumann iterates instead of unrolling. This is where the \\\"explicit gradient differentiation + reuse\\\" claim most strongly lives.\\n- **C. Differentiable functional optim, unrolled mode** — same functional API but without implicit-diff; for an apples-to-apples reuse-vs-nonreuse comparison.\\n\\nWe will run **A and B as the treatment conditions** and reserve C as an ablation only if A vs B is non-obvious.\\n\\n### 2.3 Controls (baselines)\\n\\n| Label | Method | Notes |\\n|-------|--------|-------|\\n| `VANILLA` | `loss.backward()` K times, no graph retention; hypergrad derived from final state | \\\"Standard PyTorch\\\" baseline |\\n| `AUTOGRAD_CG` | `loss.backward(create_graph=True)` once, then `torch.autograd.grad` on outer | Unrolled-through-K, known slow |\\n| `FUNCTORCH` | `torch.func.grad` (or `vmap`/`grad`) on the inner-loop functional form | Modern JAX-style baseline |\\n| `IMPL_REF` | Hand-rolled implicit diff: solve `J^T J v = g` via CG with explicit tolerance | Reference for stability ceiling |\\n| `TORCHOPT_UNROLL` | TorchOpt unrolled diff (API C above) | Isolates \\\"API surface reuse\\\" vs \\\"implicit-diff reuse\\\" |\\n| `TORCHOPT_EXPL` | TorchOpt explicit functional + reused intermediates (API A or B) | **Treatment** |\\n\\nEach cell is run with **R ≥ 30 replicates**; per-replicate seed fixed.\\n\\n### 2.4 Response variables\\n\\n**Primary — computation time**\\n- Wall-clock per outer step: `time.perf_counter()` brackets one full (K inner steps + hypergrad + outer update) round, after a 5-replicate warm-up.\\n- Wall-clock to reach fixed outer-loss tolerance (`|ΔL_outer| < 1e-6` or 100 outer steps, whichever first).\\n- Reporting: median, IQR, p95; **Wilcoxon signed-rank** paired by seed between `TORCHOPT_EXPL` and each baseline.\\n\\n**Primary — numerical stability**\\n- **W1 (convex):** analytical hypergradient is closed-form; stability = `rel_err = ||g_method − g_analytical||_2 / ||g_analytical||_2`, plus implicit-method residual `||J^T J v − g||_2` at termination.\\n- **W2 (non-convex):** treat `IMPL_REF` at tight tolerance as ground truth; stability = `rel_err = ||g_method − g_impl_ref||_2 / ||g_impl_ref||_2`.\\n- **Both:** ratio `||g_method||_2 / ||g_impl_ref||_2` across seeds as hypergrad-norm condition-number proxy.\\n- **Secondary:** outer-loss variance across seeds.\\n\\n**Secondary — memory proxy** (CPU-only, so CUDA `max_memory_allocated` is unavailable)\\n- `tracemalloc` peak around one inner solve; retained-tensor count after one outer step. Reported as proxy, not primary evidence.\\n\\n### 2.5 Hardware / dtype / threading controls\\n\\n- Threads pinned: `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1`, `torch.set_num_threads(1)` for the primary run.\\n- Threading-sensitivity sub-experiment: repeat main result at `set_num_threads(4)`.\\n- dtype: **float64** throughout (float32 float64 would confound stability signals).\\n- CPU affinity: `taskset -c 0-3` to suppress scheduler noise on the 24-core host.\\n- Locked `torch` / `torchopt` / `numpy` / `scipy` versions recorded in a manifest before any future execution.\\n- Identical `torch.manual_seed`, identical synthetic dataset per `(problem, seed)`, identical initial outer/hyper parameters across methods.\\n- **Outer-loop optimizer fixed to plain SGD** for all methods so any time difference is attributable to inner-loop differentiation, not outer-loop internals.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n1. **What \\\"explicit gradient differentiation API\\\" denotes.** I'm inferring from documented functionality (`MetaOptimizer` + `torchopt.diff.implicit`/`functional` with reused intermediates) — the workspace has no source to verify. *If this maps to a different API surface, the design pivots.*\\n2. **Expected direction & magnitude of the time effect.** I expect `TORCHOPT_EXPL` to win on W2 at K ≥ 100 and be **roughly tied or slower at K ≤ 10** (constant overhead dominates). On W1 I expect smaller gaps because the inner problem is linear and autograd is cheap. There is a real chance the win is <10% and below timing noise; the design uses paired Wilcoxon with R=30 to address this.\\n3. **Sensitivity to problem class.** Stability depends heavily on conditioning. Convex problems should favor implicit-diff methods (TorchOpt implicit path, `IMPL_REF`); unrolled methods (`AUTOGRAD_CG`, `TORCHOPT_UNROLL`) should degrade as K grows because they unroll a contractive fixed-point map into a long trajectory. W2 may show the opposite if the loss is sharply non-convex.\\n4. **Fairness of baselines.** `create_graph=True` is known to be slow and is sometimes benchmarked unfairly. We will use it in its **documented standard form** (no `allow_unused` tricks, no `torch.compile`, no graph caching hacks). For `VANILLA` we use the standard hand-rolled pattern, not a vectorized mini-loop.\\n5. **Does the API truly avoid retraversing the inner graph?** This is the load-bearing architectural claim. My understanding is that TorchOpt's explicit/implicit path reuses cached solver iterates (Neumann or fixed-point) rather than retraversing; the unrolled path must retraverse. *Source needed to confirm.*\\n6. **GPU vs CPU.** Compute is **CPU-only**. This changes the time story: dense linear solves on CPU differ from GPU, and several TorchOpt optimizations (kernel fusion, graph caching) are GPU-leaning. Memory-proxy is weaker; the experiment becomes primarily a numerical-stability + algorithmic-flop comparison with a secondary wall-clock check.\\n7. **Implicit-diff solver tolerance.** Stability of `TORCHOPT_EXPL` and `IMPL_REF` depends on the solver's tolerance / iteration cap. We will use TorchOpt defaults but **sweep tolerance ∈ {1e-3, 1e-6, 1e-9}** as a sub-experiment to bound the claim. Same for `IMPL_REF` CG tolerance.\\n8. **Inner loss form.** Matters for hypergrad magnitude and stability comparison; we fix it to **mean reduction** and call it out.\\n9. **Optim state reuse vs intermediate reuse.** \\\"Reuse\\\" can mean (a) reusing momentum/Adam buffers across K inner steps (memory win) or (b) reusing solver intermediates (compute win). The design treats these as separable mechanisms and reports them separately if possible.\\n10. **Statistical power.** R=30 paired replicates gives Wilcoxon reasonable power for ≥15% effects; smaller effects are reported as \\\"no detectable difference\\\", not \\\"no effect\\\" — important for honest framing.\\n11. **Scope / compute budget.** Full factorial × R=30 produces many runs; the design includes a **scope-reduction plan** (drop W3 first, then thread-sweep, then tolerance-sweep) if compute pressure binds.\\n12. **What is *not* in the supplied topics but is load-bearing:** (a) **single- vs multi-task** inner loop — keep single-task for a clean comparison; (b) whether outer hyperparameters are **scalar or vector** — scalar hypergrad is the analytically tractable case; (c) **dataset size** for W2 — controls inner-loop cost profile.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Decision points I need from you before drafting the full design:**\\n\\n- Confirm or correct the **TorchOpt API surface** mapping in §2.2.\\n- Approve the **problem scope**: W1+W2 only, or include W3?\\n- Is **CPU-only, single-thread primary + 4-thread secondary** acceptable given the bubblewrap environment?\\n- Should the **fairness standard** for baselines be \\\"first-idea documented usage\\\" or \\\"best-practices\\\" (per item 4)?\\n- Anything from §3 you want reframed or removed before I draft?\",\"evidence\":\"Repository inspection, executed in /workspace:\\n- `ls -la /workspace` → only `task.json` (521 bytes)\\n- `cat /workspace/task.json` → reproduced in §1; declares design-only and references a `track.md` document\\n- `ls /workspace/track.md` → \\\"No such file or directory\\\" (track.md not present)\\n- `find / -maxdepth 3 -type d \\\\( -name torchopt -o -name TorchOpt -o -name track.md -o -name src \\\\)` → only `/usr/src` and `/usr/local/src`; no TorchOpt anywhere\\n- `find / -maxdepth 4 -name \\\"*.py\\\" | grep -iE \\\"(torchopt|differentiable|metaopt)\\\"` → no matches\\n- `find /workspace -type f` → returns only `/workspace/task.json`\\n- `ls /root /tmp /home` → empty (no other materials)\\n- `python3 -c \\\"import sys; print(sys.version)\\\"` → 3.12.3\\n- `python3 -c \\\"import torch\\\"` → `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torch'`\\n- `python3 -c \\\"import torchopt\\\"` → `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torchopt'`\\nFindings consistent with task.json's \\\"repositories and compute are not required\\\" and the environment note that dependencies are not preinstalled and network is disabled.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"The scope-reduction plan (W1+W2 → drop W3 → drop thread-sweep → drop tolerance-sweep) is a contingency under compute pressure, not a planned reduction.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Statistical power is bounded at ~15% detectable differences with R=30 paired replicates; smaller effects will be presented as \\\"no detectable difference\\\", not as \\\"no effect\\\".</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"Memory findings are reported as proxies (tracemalloc peak + retained-tensor count) since CUDA memory tracking is unavailable in this environment.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"CPU-only, single-thread primary run + 4-thread sensitivity run is the chosen reproducibility control; GPU not used.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"Implicit-differentiation solver tolerance is treated as a swept hyperparameter to bound any stability claims.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"Numerical-stability interpretation fixes the precision regime to float64; otherwise stability claims would be confounded by float32 noise.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"Only public, documented TorchOpt behavior is assumed — no implementation details are leveraged that the model cannot verify.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"\\\"Standard PyTorch autograd\\\" baseline = torch.autograd.backward() without create_graph as the practitioner default; create_graph=True unrolled variant is a separate clearly-labeled baseline.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"TorchOpt API surface inferred from public documentation, not local source: \\\"explicit gradient differentiation API\\\" is mapped to torchopt.MetaOptimizer + torchopt.diff.implicit / differentiable functional optim with reused solver intermediates; alternative mappings are possible if the researcher disagrees.</item>\\n  \"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"The dataset design for W2 (size, class count, class balance) materially affects non-convex conditioning and is currently provisional.</item>\",\"$text\":\"The wall-clock measurement may be noisy on a shared CPU; pinning via taskset and OMP/MKL=1 is the planned fix, but thermal/contention effects remain.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"Any version-specific TorchOpt behavior (e.g., torch.compile hooks, xla-fallback paths) cannot be ruled out without source access.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"Whether `torch.func` is treated as a \\\"standard PyTorch autograd\\\" baseline or as a separate modern method changes the framing of the comparison.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"The implicit-diff reference (IMPL_REF) requires per-problem derivation of the inner-loop Jacobian; for W2 we will approximate it numerically with finite differences, which itself has a precision cost.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"The fairness of `create_graph=True` as a baseline is contested in the literature — paired-vs-best-practices framing could shift the time results.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"Sensitivity of the speedup to problem class is a hypothesis, not measured: a wider gap is expected for non-convex + large K than for convex + small K.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"Whether the \\\"intermediate reuse\\\" claim refers to momentum/Adam buffers, solver (Neumann/fixed-point) iterates, or autograd-graph caching — each implies a different benchmark and a different winning condition.</item>\\n  \"},\"$text\":\"The exact TorchOpt API release/version this question is targeted at is not specified; different versions expose different explicit/implicit APIs and naming has changed (MetaOptimizer → differentiable_optim).</item>\\n  \"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-4c78c3b5-a8e5-42ff-a13a-bd3e47eabc63", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
