 API\\\" claim must be sourced from publicly documented TorchOpt behavior, not local files. I will treat the relevant API surface as documented knowledge and flag every assumption about TorchOpt internals as a load-bearing uncertainty in section 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (2) Proposed approach outline\\n\\n### 2.1 Workloads (sweep across problem class)\\n\\n- W1: Ridge / linear regression on synthetic Gaussian design matrix. Loss family = convex quadratic. Inner variable = coefficients w. Outer (hyper) variable = regularizer strength lambda. Inner steps K in {10, 100, 1000}.\\n- W2: 2-layer MLP, MSE / cross-entropy on synthetic classification. Loss family = non-convex smooth. Inner variable = network params theta. Outer variable = inner learning rate alpha. Inner steps K in {10, 100, 1000}.\\n- W3 (optional): Few-shot regression (MAML-style sin-wave regression, K-shot, 5 inner steps). Loss family = non-convex smooth. Included only if W1+W2 don't already decide the question.\\n\\nInner-loop sweep K in {10, 100, 1000} is the main driver for time/memory differences: small K hides reuse gains, large K exposes them but stresses unrolled baselines. Inner optimizer: SGD-with-momentum (mu=0.9) default; Adam at K=100 sub-experiment to test sensitivity to second-moment storage.\\n\\n### 2.2 TorchOpt API surface to invoke\\n\\nBased on documented TorchOpt functionality (no local source to verify):\\n\\n- A. Explicit differentiable optimization with reused optim state — torchopt.MetaOptimizer (or MetaSGD/MetaAdam) around the inner function; momentum / Adam buffers reused across the K inner steps.\\n- B. Differentiable functional optim + implicit gradient — torchopt.diff.implicit / MetaImplicitGradient style API that reuses fixed-point / Neumann iterates instead of unrolling.\\n- C. Differentiable functional optim, unrolled mode — same functional API but without implicit-diff; apples-to-apples reuse-vs-nonreuse control.\\n\\nTreatments = A and B. Ablation only if A vs B is non-obvious.\\n\\n### 2.3 Controls (baselines)\\n\\n- VANILLA: loss.backward() K times, no graph retention; hypergrad derived from final state.\\n- AUTOGRAD_CG: loss.backward(create_graph=True) once then torch.autograd.grad on outer (unrolled-through-K, known slow).\\n- FUNCTORCH: torch.func.grad (or vmap/grad) on the inner-loop functional form.\\n- IMPL_REF: hand-rolled implicit differentiation, solving J^T J v = g via CG with explicit tolerance.\\n- TORCHOPT_UNROLL: TorchOpt unrolled diff (API C above).\\n- TORCHOPT_EXPL: TorchOpt explicit functional + reused intermediates (API A or B) — TREATMENT.\\n\\nEach cell: R >= 30 replicates, per-replicate seed fixed.\\n\\n### 2.4 Response variables\\n\\nPrimary — computation time: wall-clock per outer step (time.perf_counter brackets around one full K-inner-steps + hypergrad + outer-update round, after a 5-replicate warm-up); wall-clock to reach fixed outer-loss tolerance (|Delta L_outer| < 1e-6 or 100 outer steps, whichever first). Reporting: median, IQR, p95; Wilcoxon signed-rank paired by seed between TORCHOPT_EXPL and each baseline.\\n\\nPrimary — numerical stability: W1 uses analytical hypergradient (closed-form for ridge); metric = rel_err = ||g_method - g_analytical||_2 / ||g_analytical||_2, plus implicit-method residual ||J^T J v - g||_2 at termination. W2 treats IMPL_REF at tight tolerance as ground truth; metric = rel_err = ||g_method - g_impl_ref||_2 / ||g_impl_ref||_2. Both: ratio ||g_method||_2 / ||g_impl_ref||_2 across seeds as condition-number proxy. Secondary: outer-loss variance across seeds.\\n\\nSecondary — memory proxy: CPU-only here, so CUDA max_memory_allocated is unavailable. Use tracemalloc peak around one inner solve plus retained-tensor count after one outer step. Report as proxy, not primary evidence.\\n\\n### 2.5 Hardware / dtype / threading controls\\n\\n- Threads pinned: OMP_NUM_THREADS=1, MKL_NUM_THREADS=1, torch.set_num_threads(1) for the primary run.\\n- Threading-sensitivity sub-experiment: repeat main result at set_num_threads(4).\\n- dtype: float64 throughout (float32 would confound stability signals).\\n- CPU affinity: taskset -c 0-3 to suppress scheduler noise on the 24-core host.\\n- Locked torch / torchopt / numpy / scipy versions recorded in a manifest before any future execution.\\n- Identical torch.manual_seed per replicate, identical synthetic dataset per (problem, seed), identical initial outer / hyper parameters across methods.\\n- Outer-loop optimizer fixed to plain SGD for all methods so any time difference is attributable to inner-loop differentiation, not outer internals.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n1. The \\\"explicit gradient differentiation API\\\" is being mapped from documented TorchOpt functionality (MetaOptimizer + torchopt.diff.implicit / functional with reused intermediates); the workspace has no source to verify. If the intended API is different, the design pivots.\\n2. Expected direction & magnitude of the time effect: TORCHOPT_EXPL is expected to win on W2 at K >= 100 and be roughly tied or slower at K <= 10 (overhead dominates). On W1 the gap is expected to be small because the inner problem is linear. There is a real chance the win is sub-10% and below the timing noise floor; paired Wilcoxon with R=30 addresses this.\\n3. Sensitivity to problem class: convex problems should favor implicit-diff methods (TorchOpt implicit path, IMPL_REF); unrolled methods should degrade as K grows because they unroll a contractive fixed-point map into a long trajectory. W2 may show the opposite for sharply non-convex losses.\\n4. Fairness of baselines: create_graph=True is known to be slow and is sometimes benchmarked unfairly. Use it in its documented standard form (no allow_unused tricks, no torch.compile, no graph caching hacks). For VANILLA use the standard hand-rolled pattern, not a vectorized mini-loop.\\n5. Does the API truly avoid retraversing the inner graph? This is the load-bearing architectural claim. The understanding is that TorchOpt's explicit/implicit path reuses cached solver iterates (Neumann or fixed-point) while the unrolled path retraverses; source access would be needed to confirm.\\n6. GPU vs CPU: compute is CPU-only. Dense linear solves on CPU differ from GPU; several TorchOpt optimizations (kernel fusion, graph caching) lean GPU. Memory proxy is weaker; the experiment becomes primarily a numerical-stability + algorithmic-flop comparison with a secondary wall-clock check.\\n7. Implicit-diff solver tolerance: stability of TORCHOPT_EXPL and IMPL_REF depends on the solver's tolerance / iteration cap. Use TorchOpt defaults but sweep tolerance in {1e-3, 1e-6, 1e-9} as a sub-experiment to bound the claim. Same for IMPL_REF CG tolerance.\\n8. Inner loss form: matters for hypergrad magnitude and stability; fix to mean reduction and call it out.\\n9. Reuse can mean two distinct things: reusing momentum / Adam buffers across K inner steps (memory win) versus reusing solver intermediates (compute win). The design treats these as separable mechanisms and reports them separately when possible.\\n10. Statistical power: R=30 paired replicates gives Wilcoxon reasonable power for roughly >=15% effects; smaller effects are reported as \\\"no detectable difference\\\", not \\\"no effect\\\".\\n11. Scope / compute budget: full factorial x R=30 produces many runs; scope-reduction plan is to drop W3 first, drop thread-sweep second, drop tolerance-sweep last.\\n12. Load-bearing details not in the supplied topics: single- vs multi-task inner loop (keep single-task for a clean comparison); scalar vs vector outer hyperparameters (scalar is the analytically tractable case); dataset size for W2 (controls inner-loop cost profile).\\n\\nDecision points I need from you before drafting the full design:\\n- Confirm or correct the TorchOpt API surface mapping in section 2.2.\\n- Approve the problem scope: W1+W2 only, or include W3?\\n- Is CPU-only, single-thread primary + 4-threa