{"id": "15bee1f9-861c-4333-a7b6-947dfd386674", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:56:23.730256", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "52293aa0-5802-4050-a598-cfd1de3b9571", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-eae9315e-5ef3-4db1-b384-15a42000b459", "tool_call": {"id": "call-eae9315e-5ef3-4db1-b384-15a42000b459", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial inspection plus plan for TorchOpt vs autograd experiment\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## (1) Repository / source inspection\\n\\nWorkspace contents — what I actually saw:\\n\\n- /workspace/ contains exactly one file:\\n  - /workspace/task.json (521 bytes), contents (verbatim):\\n    {\\\"question\\\":\\\"Will the explicit gradient differentiation API in TorchOpt, which reuses intermediate data and minimizes memory allocation, reduce computation time and improve numerical stability compared to standard autograd implementations?\\\",\\\"context\\\":\\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\",\\\"documents\\\":{\\\"track.md\\\":\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"}}\\n- track.md is referenced but not present on disk (verified with ls /workspace/track.md -> \\\"No such file or directory\\\"; also absent under /root, /home, /tmp).\\n- A repo-wide search for torchopt, TorchOpt, differentiable_optim, or MetaOptimizer-related .py files returned nothing (only /usr/src and /usr/local/src matched a broad src pattern; neither contains TorchOpt).\\n- python3 is available (3.12.3) but `import torch` and `import torchopt` both raise ModuleNotFoundError — consistent with the environment note that dependencies are not preinstalled and the network is disabled.\\n- No docs, READMEs, paper PDFs, or prior design templates are present.\\n\\nImplication: the \\\"explicit gradient differentiation API\\\" claim must be sourced from publicly documented TorchOpt behavior, not local files. I treat the relevant API surface as documented knowledge and flag every assumption about TorchOpt internals as a load-bearing uncertainty in section 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (2) Proposed approach outline\\n\\n### 2.1 Workloads (sweep across problem class)\\n\\n- W1: Ridge / linear regression on a synthetic Gaussian design matrix. Convex quadratic loss. Inner variable = coefficients w; outer variable = regularizer strength lambda. Inner steps K in {10, 100, 1000}.\\n- W2: 2-layer MLP, MSE / cross-entropy on synthetic classification. Non-convex smooth loss. Inner variable = network parameters theta; outer variable = inner learning rate alpha. Inner steps K in {10, 100, 1000}.\\n- W3 (optional): Few-shot regression (MAML-style sin-wave regression, K-shot, 5 inner steps). Non-convex smooth. Included only if W1+W2 do not already decide the question.\\n\\nThe K sweep is the main driver for time/memory differences: small K hides reuse gains, large K exposes them but stresses the unrolled baseline. Inner optimizer: SGD-with-momentum (mu=0.9) by default; plus an Adam sub-experiment at K=100 to test sensitivity to second-moment storage.\\n\\n### 2.2 TorchOpt API surface to invoke\\n\\nBased on documented TorchOpt functionality (no local source to verify):\\n- A. Explicit differentiable optimization with reused optim state — torchopt.MetaOptimizer (or MetaSGD/MetaAdam) around the inner function; momentum / Adam buffers reused across the K inner steps.\\n- B. Differentiable functional optim + implicit gradient — torchopt.diff.implicit / MetaImplicitGradient style API that reuses fixed-point / Neumann iterates instead of unrolling.\\n- C. Differentiable functional optim, unrolled mode — same functional API but without implicit-diff; apples-to-apples reuse-vs-nonreuse control.\\n\\nTreatments = A and B. Use C as an ablation only if A vs B is non-obvious.\\n\\n### 2.3 Controls (baselines)\\n\\n- VANILLA: loss.backward() K times, no graph retention; hypergrad derived from final state.\\n- AUTOGRAD_CG: loss.backward(create_graph=True) once then torch.autograd.grad on outer (unrolled-through-K, known slow).\\n- FUNCTORCH: torch.func.grad (or vmap/grad) on the inner-loop functional form.\\n- IMPL_REF: hand-rolled implicit differentiation, solving J^T J v = g via CG with explicit tolerance.\\n- TORCHOPT_UNROLL: TorchOpt unrolled diff (API C above).\\n- TORCHOPT_EXPL: TorchOpt explicit functional + reused intermediates (API A or B) — TREATMENT.\\n\\nEach cell: R >= 30 replicates, per-replicate seed fixed.\\n\\n### 2.4 Response variables\\n\\nPrimary — computation time: wall-clock per outer step (time.perf_counter brackets around one full K-inner-steps + hypergrad + outer-update round, after a 5-replicate warm-up); wall-clock to reach a fixed outer-loss tolerance (|Delta L_outer| < 1e-6 or 100 outer steps, whichever first). Reporting: median, IQR, p95; Wilcoxon signed-rank paired by seed between TORCHOPT_EXPL and each baseline.\\n\\nPrimary — numerical stability: W1 uses the analytical hypergradient (closed-form for ridge); metric = rel_err = ||g_method - g_analytical||_2 / ||g_analytical||_2, plus implicit-method residual ||J^T J v - g||_2 at termination. W2 treats IMPL_REF at tight tolerance as ground truth; metric = rel_err = ||g_method - g_impl_ref||_2 / ||g_impl_ref||_2. Both: the ratio ||g_method||_2 / ||g_impl_ref||_2 across seeds as a condition-number proxy. Secondary: outer-loss variance across seeds.\\n\\nSecondary — memory proxy: CPU-only, so CUDA max_memory_allocated is unavailable. Use tracemalloc peak around one inner solve plus retained-tensor count after one outer step. Report as proxy, not primary evidence.\\n\\n### 2.5 Hardware / dtype / threading controls\\n\\n- Threads pinned: OMP_NUM_THREADS=1, MKL_NUM_THREADS=1, torch.set_num_threads(1) for the primary run.\\n- Threading sensitivity sub-experiment: repeat main result at set_num_threads(4).\\n- dtype: float64 throughout (float32 would confound stability signals).\\n- CPU affinity: taskset -c 0-3 to suppress scheduler noise on the 24-core host.\\n- Locked torch / torchopt / numpy / scipy versions recorded in a manifest before any future execution.\\n- Identical torch.manual_seed per replicate, identical synthetic dataset per (problem, seed), identical initial outer / hyper parameters across methods.\\n- Outer-loop optimizer fixed to plain SGD for all methods so any time difference is attributable to inner-loop differentiation, not outer internals.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n1. The \\\"explicit gradient differentiation API\\\" is being mapped from documented TorchOpt functionality (MetaOptimizer + torchopt.diff.implicit / functional with reused intermediates); the workspace has no source to verify. If the intended API is different, the design pivots.\\n2. Expected direction & magnitude of the time effect: TORCHOPT_EXPL is expected to win on W2 at K >= 100 and be roughly tied or slower at K <= 10 (overhead dominates). On W1 the gap is expected to be small because the inner problem is linear and autograd is cheap. There is a real chance the win is sub-10% and below the timing noise floor; paired Wilcoxon with R=30 addresses this.\\n3. Sensitivity to problem class: convex problems should favor implicit-diff methods (TorchOpt implicit path, IMPL_REF); unrolled methods should degrade as K grows because they unroll a contractive fixed-point map into a long trajectory. W2 may show the opposite for sharply non-convex losses.\\n4. Fairness of baselines: create_graph=True is known to be slow and is sometimes benchmarked unfairly. Use it in its documented standard form (no allow_unused tricks, no torch.compile, no graph caching hacks). For VANILLA use the standard hand-rolled pattern, not a vectorized mini-loop.\\n5. Does the API truly avoid retraversing the inner graph? This is the load-bearing architectural claim. The understanding is that TorchOpt's explicit/implicit path reuses cached solver iterates (Neumann or fixed-point) while the unrolled path retraverses; source access would confirm.\\n6. GPU vs CPU: compute is CPU-only. Dense linear solves on CPU differ from GPU; several TorchOpt optimizations (kernel fusion, graph caching) lean GPU. Memory proxy is weaker; the experiment becomes primarily a numerical-stability + algorithmic-flop comparison with a secondary wall-clock check.\\n7. Implicit-diff solver tolerance: stability of TORCHOPT_EXPL and IMPL_REF depends on the solver's tolerance / iteration cap. Use TorchOpt defaults but sweep tolerance in {1e-3, 1e-6, 1e-9} as a sub-experiment to bound the claim. Same for IMPL_REF CG tolerance.\\n8. Inner loss form: matters for hypergrad magnitude and stability; fix to mean reduction and call it out.\\n9. \\\"Reuse\\\" can mean two distinct things: reusing momentum / Adam buffers across K inner steps (memory win) versus reusing solver intermediates (compute win). The design treats these as separable mechanisms and reports them separately when possible.\\n10. Statistical power: R=30 paired replicates gives Wilcoxon reasonable power for roughly >=15% effects; smaller effects are reported as \\\"no detectable difference\\\", not \\\"no effect\\\".\\n11. Scope / compute budget: full factorial x R=30 produces many runs; scope-reduction plan is to drop W3 first, drop thread-sweep second, drop tolerance-sweep last.\\n12. Load-bearing details not in the supplied topics: single- vs multi-task inner loop (keep single-task for a clean comparison); scalar vs vector outer hyperparameters (scalar is the analytically tractable case); dataset size for W2 (controls inner-loop cost profile).\\n\\nDecision points I need from you before drafting the full design:\\n- Confirm or correct the TorchOpt API surface mapping in section 2.2.\\n- Approve the problem scope: W1+W2 only, or include W3?\\n- Is CPU-only, single-thread primary + 4-thread secondary acceptable given the bubblewrap environment?\\n- Should the fairness standard for baselines be \\\"first-idea documented usage\\\" or \\\"best-practices\\\" practice (per item 4)?\\n- Anything from section 3 you want reframed or removed before I draft?\",\"evidence\":\"Repository inspection, executed in /workspace:\\n- ls -la /workspace -> only task.json (521 bytes)\\n- cat /workspace/task.json -> reproduced in section 1; declares design-only and references a track.md document\\n- ls /workspace/track.md -> \\\"No such file or directory\\\" (track.md not present)\\n- find / -maxdepth 3 -type d -name torchopt -o -name TorchOpt -o -name track.md -o -name src -> only /usr/src and /usr/local/src; no TorchOpt anywhere\\n- find / -maxdepth 4 -name \\\"*.py\\\" piped to grep -iE \\\"(torchopt|differentiable|metaopt)\\\" -> no matches\\n- find /workspace -type f -> returns only /workspace/task.json\\n- ls /root /tmp /home -> empty (no other materials)\\n- python3 -c \\\"import sys; print(sys.version)\\\" -> 3.12.3\\n- python3 -c \\\"import torch\\\" -> ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torch'\\n- python3 -c \\\"import torchopt\\\" -> ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torchopt'\\nFindings consistent with task.json's \\\"repositories and compute are not required\\\" and the environment note that dependencies are not preinstalled and the network is disabled.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"The scope-reduction plan (drop W3 first, drop thread-sweep second, drop tolerance-sweep last) is a contingency under compute pressure, not a planned reduction.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Statistical power is bounded at roughly >=15% detectable differences with R=30 paired replicates; smaller effects will be presented as \\\"no detectable difference\\\", not as \\\"no effect\\\".</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Memory findings are reported as proxies (tracemalloc peak plus retained-tensor count) since CUDA memory tracking is unavailable in this environment.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"CPU-only is the chosen reproducibility control; GPU is not used. The primary run pins to a single thread with OMP_NUM_THREADS=1, MKL_NUM_THREADS=1, torch.set_num_threads=1; a 4-thread sensitivity sub-experiment is included.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Implicit-differentiation solver tolerance is treated as a swept hyperparameter to bound any stability claims.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Numerical-stability interpretation fixes the precision regime to float64; otherwise stability claims would be confounded by float32 noise.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Only public, documented TorchOpt behavior is assumed — no implementation details are leveraged that cannot be verified without local source access.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"\\\"Standard PyTorch autograd\\\" baseline is defined as torch.autograd.backward() without create_graph as the practitioner default; the create_graph=True unrolled variant is a separate clearly labeled baseline.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"TorchOpt API surface inferred from public documentation, not local source: the \\\"explicit gradient differentiation API\\\" is mapped to torchopt.MetaOptimizer plus torchopt.diff.implicit / differentiable functional optim with reused solver intermediates; alternative mappings are possible if the researcher disagrees.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"The dataset design for W2 (size, class count, class balance) materially affects non-convex conditioning and is currently provisional.</item>\",\"$text\":\"The wall-clock measurement may be noisy on a shared CPU; pinning via taskset and OMP/MKL=1 is the planned fix, but thermal and contention effects remain.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Any version-specific TorchOpt behavior (e.g., torch.compile hooks or xla-fallback paths) cannot be ruled out without source access.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether torch.func is treated as a \\\"standard PyTorch autograd\\\" baseline or as a separate modern method changes the framing of the comparison.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"The implicit-diff reference (IMPL_REF) requires per-problem derivation of the inner-loop Jacobian; for W2 we will approximate it numerically with finite differences, which itself has a precision cost.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"The fairness of create_graph=True as a baseline is contested in the literature — paired-vs-best-practices framing could shift the time results.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Sensitivity of the speedup to problem class is a hypothesis, not measured: a wider gap is expected for non-convex plus large K than for convex plus small K.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the \\\"intermediate reuse\\\" claim refers to momentum / Adam buffers, solver (Neumann / fixed-point) iterates, or autograd-graph caching — each implies a different benchmark and a different winning condition.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"The exact TorchOpt API release or version this question targets is not specified; different versions expose different explicit/implicit APIs and naming has changed (MetaOptimizer -> differentiable_optim).</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-3be81f14-9f06-493d-b68c-9a38c299198e", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
