cceptable given the bubblewrap environment?\\n- Should the fairness standard for baselines be \\\"first-idea documented usage\\\" or \\\"best-practices\\\" (per item 4)?\\n- Anything from section 3 you want reframed or removed before I draft?\",\"evidence\":\"Repository inspection, executed in /workspace:\\n- ls -la /workspace -> only task.json (521 bytes)\\n- cat /workspace/task.json -> reproduced in section 1; declares design-only and references a track.md document\\n- ls /workspace/track.md -> \\\"No such file or directory\\\" (track.md not present)\\n- find / -maxdepth 3 -type d \\\\( -name torchopt -o -name TorchOpt -o -name track.md -o -name src \\\\) -> only /usr/src and /usr/local/src; no TorchOpt anywhere\\n- find / -maxdepth 4 -name \\\"*.py\\\" piped to grep -iE \\\"(torchopt|differentiable|metaopt)\\\" -> no matches\\n- find /workspace -type f -> returns only /workspace/task.json\\n- ls /root /tmp /home -> empty (no other materials)\\n- python3 -c \\\"import sys; print(sys.version)\\\" -> 3.12.3\\n- python3 -c \\\"import torch\\\" -> ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torch'\\n- python3 -c \\\"import torchopt\\\" -> ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torchopt'\\nFindings consistent with task.json's \\\"repositories and compute are not required\\\" and the environment note that dependencies are not preinstalled and network is disabled.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"9. The scope-reduction plan (drop W3 first, drop thread-sweep second, drop tolerance-sweep last) is a contingency under compute pressure, not a planned reduction.</item>\",\"$text\":\"8. Statistical power is bounded at roughly >=15% detectable differences with R=30 paired replicates; smaller effects are presented as \\\"no detectable difference\\\", not as \\\"no effect\\\".</item>\"},\"$text\":\"7. Memory findings are reported as proxies (tracemalloc peak plus retained-tensor count) since CUDA memory tracking is unavailable in this environment.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"6. CPU-only is the chosen reproducibility control; GPU is not used. The primary run pins to a single thread with OMP_NUM_THREADS=1, MKL_NUM_THREADS=1, torch.set_num_threads=1; a 4-thread sensitivity sub-experiment is included.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"5. Implicit-differentiation solver tolerance is treated as a swept hyperparameter (1e-3 / 1e-6 / 1e-9) to bound any stability claims; same sweep applies to IMPL_REF CG tolerance.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"4. Numerical-stability interpretation fixes the precision regime to float64; otherwise stability claims would be confounded by float32 noise.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"3. Only public, documented TorchOpt behavior is assumed — no implementation details are leveraged that cannot be verified without local source access.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"2. \\\"Standard PyTorch autograd\\\" baseline is defined as torch.autograd.backward() without create_graph as the practitioner default; the create_graph=True unrolled variant is a separate clearly labeled baseline.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"1. TorchOpt API surface mapping from public documentation: \\\"explicit gradient differentiation API\\\" is treated as torchopt.MetaOptimizer plus torchopt.diff.implicit / differentiable functional optim with reused solver intermediates; alternative mappings are possible if the researcher disagrees.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"9. The dataset design for W2 (size, class count, class balance) materially affects non-convex conditioning and is currently provisional.</item>\",\"$text\":\"8. The wall-clock measurement may be noisy on a shared CPU; pinning via taskset and OMP/MKL=1 is the planned fix, but thermal and contention effects remain.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"7. Any version-specific TorchOpt behavior (e.g., torch.compile hooks or xla-fallback paths) cannot be ruled out without source access.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"6. Whether torch.func is treated as a \\\"standard PyTorch autograd\\\" baseline or as a separate modern method changes the framing of the comparison.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"5. The implicit-diff reference (IMPL_REF) requires per-problem derivation of the inner-loop Jacobian; for W2 we will approximate it numerically with finite differences, which itself has a precision cost.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"4. The fairness of create_graph=True as a baseline is contested in the literature — paired-vs-best-practices framing could shift the time results.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"3. Sensitivity of the speedup to problem class is a hypothesis, not measured: a wider gap is expected for non-convex plus large K than for convex plus small K.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"2. Whether the \\\"intermediate reuse\\\" claim refers to momentum / Adam buffers, solver (Neumann / fixed-point) iterates, or autograd-graph caching — each implies a different benchmark and a different winning condition.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"1. The exact TorchOpt API release or version this question targets is not specified; different versions expose different explicit/implicit APIs and naming has changed (MetaOptimizer -> differentiable_optim).</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-4a92f2de-e74a-4578-8387-2f15bcb11ec8", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 24}
