usr/share/dict/README.select-wordlist\\n/usr/share/ipp-usb/quirks/README\\n/usr/share/alsa/ucm/README.md\\n/usr/share/alsa/ucm2/DEBUG.md\\n/usr/share/alsa/ucm2/README.md\\n/usr/local/cuda-13.1/README\\n/usr/local/cuda-13.1/gds/README\\n(end)\\n\\n=== python / module status ===\\npython 3.12.3 (main, Aug 31 2026, 10:18:26) [GCC 13.3.0]\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n\\n=== cpu info ===\\n24\\n24\\n\\n=== home/root/tmp ===\\nls: cannot access '/root': No such file or directory\\n/home/actor:\\ntotal 0\\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 1776737281 748400513 40 Oct 23 01:59 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 1776737281 748400513 60 Oct 23 01:59 ..\\n\\n/tmp:\\ntotal 0\\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 1776737281 748400513  40 Oct 23 01:59 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 1776737281 748400513 240 Oct 23 01:59 ..\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "02990732-9127-43fd-8a6b-7e8579be0c03", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 40}
{"id": "bff3de41-62fd-455c-ac7c-f4d1d29c31da", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T19:00:13.394742", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "3611414c-7c73-49c8-b325-95ea2f7f2f7c", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## (1) Repository / source inspection — observations only\n\nCommands run and what they produced:\n\n- `ls -la /workspace` → exactly one entry: `-rw------- 1 ... 521 ... task.json` (no other files, no subdirectories).\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` → `task.json` is the only file under `/workspace`.\n- `find / -iname '*torchopt*'` across the entire filesystem → zero results. No TorchOpt source tree, no TorchOpt package, no TorchOpt pip wheel anywhere.\n- `find / -name '*.md'` and `find / -iname 'README*'`, excluding system /usr/share/doc, /usr/lib, /proc, /sys → only unrelated system files; nothing related to TorchOpt.\n- `/workspace/track.md` is referenced inside `task.json` (`\"documents\": {\"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"}`) but does not exist on disk.\n- Module availability: `python3` is 3.12.3. `import torch` → ModuleNotFoundError. `import torchopt` → ModuleNotFoundError. `import numpy` → ModuleNotFoundError.\n- Environment notes from `task.json` and the wrapper context: runtime = bubblewrap, network = disabled, no preinstalled project deps, GPU not enabled (CPU only), 24 logical CPUs available (`nproc` = 24).\n- I will not fabricate TorchOpt API names, function signatures, or quoted claims from documentation or papers. The only TorchOpt-specific facts known to me come from general public knowledge and are flagged below as inferences that must be confirmed against an actual TorchOpt source checkout before execution.\n\nWhat is simultaneously true and recorded as facts (not blockers to be papered over):\n- TorchOpt source is **not** present in this workspace.\n- TorchOpt is **not** installable here, because (a) the package is **not** preinstalled, (b) PyTorch is **not** preinstalled, (c) network is disabled, so neither `pip install torchopt` nor cloning the GitHub repository will work from inside this environment.\n- The intended design therefore can only be specified at this stage; bench data would have to come from an environment where TorchOpt and PyTorch are present.\n\n---\n\n## (2) Proposed approach outline\n\n### 2.1 Tasks (differentiation workloads) under comparison\n\n- **Task T1 — convex regression inner loop (clean ground truth)**: ridge regression on a synthetic Gaussian design matrix. Inner loss is convex quadratic in the inner parameters `w`; outer variable is the regularizer strength `λ`. The hypergradient w.r.t. `λ` is available analytically (closed form). Primary purpose: stable reference for the *numerical-stability* response variable. Inner-step sweep `K ∈ {10, 100, 1000}`.\n- **Task T2 — non-convex MLP inner loop**: 2-layer MLP, cross-entropy on a synthetic classification problem. Outer variable is the inner learning rate `α`. No analytical reference → stability measured against a high-tolerance implicit-diff or finite-difference reference. Primary purpose: realistic non-convex load where implicit-diff is most plausibly advantageous. Inner-step sweep `K ∈ {10, 100, 1000}`.\n- **Optional Task T3 — small CNN inner loop**: 2-3 layer CNN on Fashion-MNIST (or synthetic images if no dataset deps). Included only if T1+T2 leave the stability axis underdetermined; documented as drop-first in §3 scope reduction.\n- Inner optimizer: SGD with momentum (μ = 0.9) by default; an Adam sub-experiment at K = 100 to probe sensitivity to second-moment state.\n\n### 2.2 TorchOpt API surface — candidate entry points to be source-confirmed\n\nEach name below is a candidate to be verified against an actual TorchOpt source checkout before execution; the public TorchOpt README documents these in general terms but I have NOT cross-checked signatures or behaviour against the installed source (none available here).\n\n- **Treatment A — implicit-diff functional API**: the documented entry point is `torchopt.diff.implicit.grad` (or equivalent sub-module), which returns a hypergradient via an implicit-differentiation fixed-point / linear-system solve. The reuse-and-minimize-allocation claim is most plausibly local to this path.\n- **Treatment B — MetaOptimizer family**: `torchopt.MetaOptimizer` (and subclasses such as MetaSGD / MetaAdam). State is owned by the optimizer object; treat the iteration-equivalent to running an inner loop wrapped in a `MetaOptimizer` so that the optimizer's state is persistent across inner steps.\n- Optional ablation C — `torchopt.diff.functional` (functional, unrolled path) to isolate \"API surface reuse\" vs \"implicit-diff reuse\".\n\nImportant caveat to record before approval: the **exact** module path, function name, and solver arguments in current TorchOpt must be checked in the source. None of the names above are quoted from local files.\n\n### 2.3 Autograd / control baselines\n\n- **C1 — vanilla `torch.autograd`**: a hand-written inner loop with `loss.backward()` (no `create_graph`) repeated for K steps, hypergradient computed by-hand from the final state.\n- **C2 — autograd with `create_graph=True`**: unrolled through K inner steps, then `torch.autograd.grad` on the outer loss. The \"fair naive\" autograd benchmark; will use the documented standard form (no `allow_unused` tricks, no `torch.compile`, no graph caching hacks).\n- **C3 — `torch.func` / `functorch`**: `torch.func.grad` or `functorch.jacrev` on the inner-loop functional form. This is the modern JAX-style baseline.\n- **C4 — hand-rolled implicit-diff reference**: solve the inner loop's implicit equation directly (CG / Neumann series on `(I − ∂step/∂w)` or the equivalent linear system at fixed tolerance). Purpose: numerical-stability ceiling, not a speed baseline. (Naming this a *reference*, not a *baseline*.)\n\n### 2.4 Response variables and operationalization\n\n**Primary — computation time (per inner solve).**\n- Instrumentation: `time.perf_counter_ns()` brackets around one full (K-step inner solve + hypergrad compute) round. Note that `torch.cuda.Event` is **not** available because GPU is disabled in this environment; this CPU/CUDA difference is itself a load-bearing detail (see §3). Warm-up: 5 discarded rounds per cell. Timed: R ≥ 30 rounds per (task, method, K) cell, fixed seed per round.\n- Reporting: median, IQR (p25/p75), p95 wall-clock per inner solve in milliseconds; Wilcoxon signed-rank test paired by seed for `Treatment A` and `Treatment B` vs each of C1/C2/C3 separately.\n- Secondary time metric: total wall-clock to reach a fixed outer-loss tolerance `|ΔL_outer| < 1e-6` or a 100-outer-step cap (whichever comes first).\n\n**Primary — numerical stability.**\n- **T1 (convex, analytical ground truth):** compute `g_ref = dL_outer/dλ` analytically. Stability = `rel_err(λ) = ‖g_method − g_ref‖₂ / ‖g_ref‖₂`. For implicit-diff methods, additionally report the residual `‖A v − g‖₂` of the inner solve at termination, and the condition number `κ(A)` of the implicit-system matrix. The `κ(A)` itself is the second stability proxy.\n- **T2 (non-convex):** use C4 at tight tolerance (or finite-difference with central differences at step ≈ 1e-5 in float64) as the reference. Stability = `rel_err(method, ref) = ‖g_method − g_ref‖₂ / ‖g_ref‖₂`. Also report condition number of the implicit-system matrix if Treatment A's solve exposes it.\n- Secondary stability metric: outer-loss variance across R seeds (a robust but coarse signal).\n\n**Secondary — memory proxy.**\n- CPU-only here, so `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated` is unavailable. Use `tracemalloc` peak across one inner solve, plus a static count of retained intermediate tensors (e.g., number of `.grad` fields held). Report as **proxy only**, not primary evidence, and label as such.\n\n### 2.5 Held-constant environment\n\n- **dtype**: float64 throughout. float32 would confound the stability claim via round-off; do not allow it as an option in this design.\n- **device**: CPU only (GPU is disabled in the environment).\n- **threads / BLAS**: pin `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1`, `torch.set_num_threads(1)` for the primary run. Repeat the main result at `set_num_threads(4)` as a single-axis threading sensitivity check (drop-second if compute budget binds; see §3).\n- **BLAS choice**: match what PyTorch's CPU build ships (OpenBLAS or MKL depending on the build) — DO NOT swap mid-experiment; record the version.\n- **Versions**: lock and record exact `torch`, `torchopt`, `numpy`, `scipy` versions in a manifest.\n- **Seeds**: identical `torch.manual_seed` per replicate, identical synthetic dataset per (task, seed), identical initial outer and outer-meta parameters across methods.\n- **Inner solver cap**: identical tolerance and iteration cap for every implicit-diff method (TorchOpt defaults, plus a swept tolerance sub-experiment at {1e-3, 1e-6, 1e-9} — drop-third under budget pressure).\n- **Outer-loop optimizer**: identical plain SGD across all methods so any time difference is attributable to inner-loop differentiation.\n- **Inner loss reduction**: mean (matters for hypergradient magnitude — fix and call out).\n- **Inner step K**: identical across methods within a cell.\n- Repeated nproc=24 host: pin outer CPU affinity via `taskset -c 0-3` to suppress scheduler noise.\n\n---\n\n## (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties for your review\n\n1. **\"Reuse of intermediate data / minimize memory allocation\" — does the claim extend to the hypergradient (outer meta-parameter gradient) or only to inner-parameter gradients?** This is your flagged critical question. My prior is that the reuse claim **does** extend to the hypergradient path (otherwise the public framing of TorchOpt for meta-learning makes little sense), but the inner-parameter reuse is a separate, plausibly larger effect. The design separates these via Treatment A (implicit-diff full hypergrad), Treatment B (`MetaOptimizer`-style inner-state reuse), and ablation C (TorchOpt unrolled, same outer gradient path but no implicit-diff reuse). I want this point checked against the actual module before locking the design.\n\n2. **Direction and likely magnitude of the speed-up.** A priori expectation:\n   - For C2 (`create_graph=True` unrolled) vs Treatment A: I expect Treatment A to be faster, with the gap widening roughly with K. Magnitude is conjectural; the public TorchOpt README reports substantial speed-ups over unrolled `create_graph=True` autograd, but I have not re-derived those numbers and they were measured on GPU.\n   - For C1 vs Treatment B: smaller gap; C1 is already cheap (no graph retention), and Treatment B's win is mostly memory-side on GPU.\n   - For C3 (`torch.func`) vs Treatment A: C3 may be competitive; this is the most informative comparison.\n   - I will not promise a specific speed-up factor.\n\n3. **Sensitivity to problem class (convex vs non-convex).** The numerical-stability advantage of implicit-diff is largest when the inner-step fixed-point map is contractive; that contractivity holds more generally for T1 (well-conditioned convex quadratic with reasonable step size) and degrades for T2 (non-convex) at larger inner step sizes. So I expect the stability win on T1 > T2 at small/medium K, and the layout may flip at extreme K where unrolled methods compound round-off. The `(T1, T2) × {10, 100, 1000}` grid is designed to expose this.\n\n4. **Directionality of the stability claim.** Numerical stability could improve in two distinct ways — (a) smaller relative error vs reference, (b) lower variance of the hypergradient across seeds. Whether TorchOpt improves (a), (b), or both is an empirical open question the design reports separately.\n\n5. **CPU-only environment invalidates `torch.cuda.Event` timing.** gpu_enabled is false in this environment. The CPU-side wall-clock timing is a strictly weaker measurement because (i) PyTorch's CPU dispatch path differs significantly from its CUDA path, (ii) several optimizations TorchOpt uses (kernel fusion, graph caching) are GPU-leaning. The experiment becomes primarily a numerical-stability + algorithmic-flop comparison with a secondary wall-clock check. This is a load-bearing constraint, not a footnote.\n\n6. **BLAS and threading interaction.** A 24-core CPU with a non-deterministic thread count can swamp genuine algorithmic differences with thread-scheduling noise. The pinning above addresses this but does not eliminate it; I will report timing with and without `taskset` pinning as a one-axis sensitivity.\n\n7. **What `create_graph=True` is genuinely fair.** I will use it in its documented standard form, without clever tricks (no `allow_unused`, no `torch.compile`, no manual activation checkpointing). If your fairness bar is practitioner-best-practices rather than first-idea-documented-usage, the time results may shift meaningfully in either direction. Both framings can be run, but the headline result will be reported under the documented-standard form.\n\n8. **Implicit-diff solver tolerance is a hyperparameter of the experiment, not just of the method.** The same `(task, K)` cell with `tol = 1e-3` and `tol = 1e-9` will give different speed and different stability numbers. I will sweep this once, not multiple times nested inside other axes.\n\n9. **Single-task vs multi-task inner loop.** Single-task keeps the comparison clean (one closure of the inner loop, one hypergradient). A multi-task (MAML-flavoured) inner loop is dropped (T3 is optional and single-task if used) to avoid confusing \"outer gradient through task-averaging\" with the TorchOpt reuse claim.\n\n10. **Hyperparameter dimensionality.** Outer hyperparameters are scalars (single `λ` for T1, single `α` for T2) — vector-valued hyperparams would change the implicit-diff linear system conditioning profile. Scalar keeps the comparison tractable and matches the analytical reference for T1.\n\n11. **Scope-reduction plan if compute budget binds.** Drop order: T3 → threading-sensitivity sweep → solver-tolerance sweep → Adam sub-experiment. Not planned reductions; contingencies.\n\n12. **Source contamination risk.** Several TorchOpt API names and behaviours are referenced from public documentation at large. Names like `torchopt.diff.implicit.grad`, `MetaOptimizer`, `differentiable_optim`, and the specific reuse-claim wording may have shifted across versions. **None of these are confirmed against an installed source in this workspace and they must be re-verified at execution time.** If verification fails, the design pivots rather than re-labelling a different API as \"the TorchOpt one\".\n\n13. **Network/install constraints.** Even if this design is approved, no execution can happen in this environment as configured (`torch` and `torchopt` are not installed and the network is disabled). Bench results, if ever produced, will originate from a separate environment with these dependencies available, and the source-confirmation in item 12 must happen there.\n\n---\n\n**Decisions I need from you before drafting the full design:**\n- Confirm or correct the labelling of T1/T2/T3 and the candidate API names in §2.1–§2.2.\n- Confirm scope: T1+T2 only, or include T3?\n- Are the held-constant environment pin choices (float64, CPU, single-thread primary + 4-thread sensitivity, identical solver tolerance) acceptable?\n- Is the fairness standard for `create_graph=True` baselines \"first-idea documented usage\" or \"best-practices\" (per item 7)?\n- Is recording the time difference as secondary and the stability difference as primary acceptable, given the CPU-only environment (per item 5)?\n- Anything in §3 you want reframed, especially the answer to your flagged question in §3 item 1?", "evidence": "Repository inspection, executed inside /workspace and across the filesystem:\n- ls -la /workspace -> one entry only: -rw------- ... 521 ... task.json (no subdirectories).\n- find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -> only task.json.\n- find / -iname '*torchopt*' (case-insensitive, maxdepth 5) -> zero results across the entire filesystem.\n- find / -name '*.md' and -iname 'README*', excluding /usr/share/doc, /usr/lib, /proc, /sys -> unrelated system files only; no TorchOpt docs or README.\n- ls /workspace/track.md -> \"No such file or directory\"; track.md referenced inside task.json is absent.\n- python3 -c \"import sys; print(sys.version)\" -> 3.12.3.\n- python3 -c \"import torch\" -> ModuleNotFoundError.\n- python3 -c \"import torchopt\" -> ModuleNotFoundError.\n- python3 -c \"import numpy\" -> ModuleNotFoundError.\n- nproc -> 24; /proc/cpuinfo -> 24 logical processors.\n- /home/actor and /tmp are empty; /root does not exist.\n- task.json self-declares \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- Environment summary: runtime=bubblewrap, network=disabled, gpu_enabled=false.\nExplicitly NOT done: no TorchOpt source was inspected (none present); no TorchOpt API names were quoted from local files; no installation attempt was made (network disabled, no preinstalled deps).", "assumptions": [], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-810d0112-89d1-414b-831f-9bbb3d198753", "tool_call": {"id": "call-810d0112-89d1-414b-831f-9bbb3d198753", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Real initial inspection and approach plan for TorchOpt vs autograd experiment\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"## (1) Repository / source inspection \\u2014 observations only\\n\\nCommands run and what they produced:\\n\\n- `ls -la /workspace` \\u2192 exactly one entry: `-rw------- 1 ... 521 ... task.json` (no other files, no subdirectories).\\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` \\u2192 `task.json` is the only file under `/workspace`.\\n- `find / -iname '*torchopt*'` across the entire filesystem \\u2192 zero results. No TorchOpt source tree, no TorchOpt package, no TorchOpt pip wheel anywhere.\\n- `find / -name '*.md'` and `find / -iname 'README*'`, excluding system /usr/share/doc, /usr/lib, /proc, /sys \\u2192 only unrelated system files; nothing related to TorchOpt.\\n- `/workspace/track.md` is referenced inside `task.json` (`\\\"documents\\\": {\\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"}`) but does not exist on disk.\\n- Module availability: `python3` is 3.12.3. `import torch` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError. `import torchopt` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError. `import numpy` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError.\\n- Environment notes from `task.json` and the wrapper context: runtime = bubblewrap, network = disabled, no preinstalled project deps, GPU not enabled (CPU only), 24 logical CPUs available (`nproc` = 24).\\n- I will not fabricate TorchOpt API names, function signatures, or quoted claims from documentation or papers. The only TorchOpt-specific facts known to me come from general public knowledge and are flagged below as inferences that must be confirmed against an actual TorchOpt source checkout before execution.\\n\\nWhat is simultaneously true and recorded as facts (not blockers to be papered over):\\n- TorchOpt source is **not** present in this workspace.\\n- TorchOpt is **not** installable here, because (a) the package is **not** preinstalled, (b) PyTorch is **not** preinstalled, (c) network is disabled, so neither `pip install torchopt` nor cloning the GitHub repository will work from inside this environment.\\n- The intended design therefore can only be specified at this stage; bench data would have to come from an environment where TorchOpt and PyTorch are present.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (2) Proposed approach outline\\n\\n### 2.1 Tasks (differentiation workloads) under comparison\\n\\n- **Task T1 \\u2014 convex regression inner loop (clean ground truth)**: ridge regression on a synthetic Gaussian design matrix. Inner loss is convex quadratic in the inner parameters `w`; outer variable is the regularizer strength `\\u03bb`. The hypergradient w.r.t. `\\u03bb` is available analytically (closed form). Primary purpose: stable reference for the *numerical-stability* response variable. Inner-step sweep `K \\u2208 {10, 100, 1000}`.\\n- **Task T2 \\u2014 non-convex MLP inner loop**: 2-layer MLP, cross-entropy on a synthetic classification problem. Outer variable is the inner learning rate `\\u03b1`. No analytical reference \\u2192 stability measured against a high-tolerance implicit-diff or finite-difference reference. Primary purpose: realistic non-convex load where implicit-diff is most plausibly advantageous. Inner-step sweep `K \\u2208 {10, 100, 1000}`.\\n- **Optional Task T3 \\u2014 small CNN inner loop**: 2-3 layer CNN on Fashion-MNIST (or synthetic images if no dataset deps). Included only if T1+T2 leave the stability axis underdetermined; documented as drop-first in \\u00a73 scope reduction.\\n- Inner optimizer: SGD with momentum (\\u03bc = 0.9) by default; an Adam sub-experiment at K = 100 to probe sensitivity to second-moment state.\\n\\n### 2.2 TorchOpt API surface \\u2014 candidate entry points to be source-confirmed\\n\\nEach name below is a candidate to be verified against an actual TorchOpt source checkout before execution; the public TorchOpt README documents these in general terms but I have NOT cross-checked signatures or behaviour against the installed source (none available here).\\n\\n- **Treatment A \\u2014 implicit-diff functional API**: the documented entry point is `torchopt.diff.implicit.grad` (or equivalent sub-module), which returns a hypergradient via an implicit-differentiation fixed-point / linear-system solve. The reuse-and-minimize-allocation claim is most plausibly local to this path.\\n- **Treatment B \\u2014 MetaOptimizer family**: `torchopt.MetaOptimizer` (and subclasses such as MetaSGD / MetaAdam). State is owned by the optimizer object; treat the iteration-equivalent to running an inner loop wrapped in a `MetaOptimizer` so that the optimizer's state is persistent across inner steps.\\n- Optional ablation C \\u2014 `torchopt.diff.functional` (functional, unrolled path) to isolate \\\"API surface reuse\\\" vs \\\"implicit-diff reuse\\\".\\n\\nImportant caveat to record before approval: the **exact** module path, function name, and solver arguments in current TorchOpt must be checked in the source. None of the names above are quoted from local files.\\n\\n### 2.3 Autograd / control baselines\\n\\n- **C1 \\u2014 vanilla `torch.autograd`**: a hand-written inner loop with `loss.backward()` (no `create_graph`) repeated for K steps, hypergradient computed by-hand from the final state.\\n- **C2 \\u2014 autograd with `create_graph=True`**: unrolled through K inner steps, then `torch.autograd.grad` on the outer loss. The \\\"fair naive\\\" autograd benchmark; will use the documented standard form (no `allow_unused` tricks, no `torch.compile`, no graph caching hacks).\\n- **C3 \\u2014 `torch.func` / `functorch`**: `torch.func.grad` or `functorch.jacrev` on the inner-loop functional form. This is the modern JAX-style baseline.\\n- **C4 \\u2014 hand-rolled implicit-diff reference**: solve the inner loop's implicit equation directly (CG / Neumann series on `(I \\u2212 \\u2202step/\\u2202w)` or the equivalent linear system at fixed tolerance). Purpose: numerical-stability ceiling, not a speed baseline. (Naming this a *reference*, not a *baseline*.)\\n\\n### 2.4 Response variables and operationalization\\n\\n**Primary \\u2014 computation time (per inner solve).**\\n- Instrumentation: `time.perf_counter_ns()` brackets around one full (K-step inner solve + hypergrad compute) round. Note that `torch.cuda.Event` is **not** available because GPU is disabled in this environment; this CPU/CUDA difference is itself a load-bearing detail (see \\u00a73). Warm-up: 5 discarded rounds per cell. Timed: R \\u2265 30 rounds per (task, method, K) cell, fixed seed per round.\\n- Reporting: median, IQR (p25/p75), p95 wall-clock per inner solve in milliseconds; Wilcoxon signed-rank test paired by seed for `Treatment A` and `Treatment B` vs each of C1/C2/C3 separately.\\n- Secondary time metric: total wall-clock to reach a fixed outer-loss tolerance `|\\u0394L_outer| < 1e-6` or a 100-outer-step cap (whichever comes first).\\n\\n**Primary \\u2014 numerical stability.**\\n- **T1 (convex, analytical ground truth):** compute `g_ref = dL_outer/d\\u03bb` analytically. Stability = `rel_err(\\u03bb) = \\u2016g_method \\u2212 g_ref\\u2016\\u2082 / \\u2016g_ref\\u2016\\u2082`. For implicit-diff methods, additionally report the residual `\\u2016A v \\u2212 g\\u2016\\u2082` of the inner solve at termination, and the condition number `\\u03ba(A)` of the implicit-system matrix. The `\\u03ba(A)` itself is the second stability proxy.\\n- **T2 (non-convex):** use C4 at tight tolerance (or finite-difference with central differences at step \\u2248 1e-5 in float64) as the reference. Stability = `rel_err(method, ref) = \\u2016g_method \\u2212 g_ref\\u2016\\u2082 / \\u2016g_ref\\u2016\\u2082`. Also report condition number of the implicit-system matrix if Treatment A's solve exposes it.\\n- Secondary stability metric: outer-loss variance across R seeds (a robust but coarse signal).\\n\\n**Secondary \\u2014 memory proxy.**\\n- CPU-only here, so `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated` is unavailable. Use `tracemalloc` peak across one inner solve, plus a static count of retained intermediate tensors (e.g., number of `.grad` fields held). Report as **proxy only**, not primary evidence, and label as such.\\n\\n### 2.5 Held-constant environment\\n\\n- **dtype**: float64 throughout. float32 would confound the stability claim via round-off; do not allow it as an option in this design.\\n- **device**: CPU only (GPU is disabled in the environment).\\n- **threads / BLAS**: pin `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1`, `torch.set_num_threads(1)` for the primary run. Repeat the main result at `set_num_threads(4)` as a single-axis threading sensitivity check (drop-second if compute budget binds; see \\u00a73).\\n- **BLAS choice**: match what PyTorch's CPU build ships (OpenBLAS or MKL depending on the build) \\u2014 DO NOT swap mid-experiment; record the version.\\n- **Versions**: lock and record exact `torch`, `torchopt`, `numpy`, `scipy` versions in a manifest.\\n- **Seeds**: identical `torch.manual_seed` per replicate, identical synthetic dataset per (task, seed), identical initial outer and outer-meta parameters across methods.\\n- **Inner solver cap**: identical tolerance and iteration cap for every implicit-diff method (TorchOpt defaults, plus a swept tolerance sub-experiment at {1e-3, 1e-6, 1e-9} \\u2014 drop-third under budget pressure).\\n- **Outer-loop optimizer**: identical plain SGD across all methods so any time difference is attributable to inner-loop differentiation.\\n- **Inner loss reduction**: mean (matters for hypergradient magnitude \\u2014 fix and call out).\\n- **Inner step K**: identical across methods within a cell.\\n- Repeated nproc=24 host: pin outer CPU affinity via `taskset -c 0-3` to suppress scheduler noise.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties for your review\\n\\n1. **\\\"Reuse of intermediate data / minimize memory allocation\\\" \\u2014 does the claim extend to the hypergradient (outer meta-parameter gradient) or only to inner-parameter gradients?** This is your flagged critical question. My prior is that the reuse claim **does** extend to the hypergradient path (otherwise the public framing of TorchOpt for meta-learning makes little sense), but the inner-parameter reuse is a separate, plausibly larger effect. The design separates these via Treatment A (implicit-diff full hypergrad), Treatment B (`MetaOptimizer`-style inner-state reuse), and ablation C (TorchOpt unrolled, same outer gradient path but no implicit-diff reuse). I want this point checked against the actual module before locking the design.\\n\\n2. **Direction and likely magnitude of the speed-up.** A priori expectation:\\n   - For C2 (`create_graph=True` unrolled) vs Treatment A: I expect Treatment A to be faster, with the gap widening roughly with K. Magnitude is conjectural; the public TorchOpt README reports substantial speed-ups over unrolled `create_graph=True` autograd, but I have not re-derived those numbers and they were measured on GPU.\\n   - For C1 vs Treatment B: smaller gap; C1 is already cheap (no graph retention), and Treatment B's win is mostly memory-side on GPU.\\n   - For C3 (`torch.func`) vs Treatment A: C3 may be competitive; this is the most informative comparison.\\n   - I will not promise a specific speed-up factor.\\n\\n3. **Sensitivity to problem class (convex vs non-convex).** The numerical-stability advantage of implicit-diff is largest when the inner-step fixed-point map is contractive; that contractivity holds more generally for T1 (well-conditioned convex quadratic with reasonable step size) and degrades for T2 (non-convex) at larger inner step sizes. So I expect the stability win on T1 > T2 at small/medium K, and the layout may flip at extreme K where unrolled methods compound round-off. The `(T1, T2) \\u00d7 {10, 100, 1000}` grid is designed to expose this.\\n\\n4. **Directionality of the stability claim.** Numerical stability could improve in two distinct ways \\u2014 (a) smaller relative error vs reference, (b) lower variance of the hypergradient across seeds. Whether TorchOpt improves (a), (b), or both is an empirical open question the design reports separately.\\n\\n5. **CPU-only environment invalidates `torch.cuda.Event` timing.** gpu_enabled is false in this environment. The CPU-side wall-clock timing is a strictly weaker measurement because (i) PyTorch's CPU dispatch path differs significantly from its CUDA path, (ii) several optimizations TorchOpt uses (kernel fusion, graph caching) are GPU-leaning. The experiment becomes primarily a numerical-stability + algorithmic-flop comparison with a secondary wall-clock check. This is a load-bearing constraint, not a footnote.\\n\\n6. **BLAS and threading interaction.** A 24-core CPU with a non-deterministic thread count can swamp genuine algorithmic differences with thread-scheduling noise. The pinning above addresses this but does not eliminate it; I will report timing with and without `taskset` pinning as a one-axis sensitivity.\\n\\n7. **What `create_graph=True` is genuinely fair.** I will use it in its documented standard form, without clever tricks (no `allow_unused`, no `torch.compile`, no manual activation checkpointing). If your fairness bar is practitioner-best-practices rather than first-idea-documented-usage, the time results may shift meaningfully in either direction. Both framings can be run, but the headline result will be reported under the documented-standard form.\\n\\n8. **Implicit-diff solver tolerance is a hyperparameter of the experiment, not just of the method.** The same `(task, K)` cell with `tol = 1e-3` and `tol = 1e-9` will give different speed and different stability numbers. I will sweep this once, not multiple times nested inside other axes.\\n\\n9. **Single-task vs multi-task inner loop.** Single-task keeps the comparison clean (one closure of the inner loop, one hypergradient). A multi-task (MAML-flavoured) inner loop is dropped (T3 is optional and single-task if used) to avoid confusing \\\"outer gradient through task-averaging\\\" with the TorchOpt reuse claim.\\n\\n10. **Hyperparameter dimensionality.** Outer hyperparameters are scalars (single `\\u03bb` for T1, single `\\u03b1` for T2) \\u2014 vector-valued hyperparams would change the implicit-diff linear system conditioning profile. Scalar keeps the comparison tractable and matches the analytical reference for T1.\\n\\n11. **Scope-reduction plan if compute budget binds.** Drop order: T3 \\u2192 threading-sensitivity sweep \\u2192 solver-tolerance sweep \\u2192 Adam sub-experiment. Not planned reductions; contingencies.\\n\\n12. **Source contamination risk.** Several TorchOpt API names and behaviours are referenced from public documentation at large. Names like `torchopt.diff.implicit.grad`, `MetaOptimizer`, `differentiable_optim`, and the specific reuse-claim wording may have shifted across versions. **None of these are confirmed against an installed source in this workspace and they must be re-verified at execution time.** If verification fails, the design pivots rather than re-labelling a different API as \\\"the TorchOpt one\\\".\\n\\n13. **Network/install constraints.** Even if this design is approved, no execution can happen in this environment as configured (`torch` and `torchopt` are not installed and the network is disabled). Bench results, if ever produced, will originate from a separate environment with these dependencies available, and the source-confirmation in item 12 must happen there.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Decisions I need from you before drafting the full design:**\\n- Confirm or correct the labelling of T1/T2/T3 and the candidate API names in \\u00a72.1\\u2013\\u00a72.2.\\n- Confirm scope: T1+T2 only, or include T3?\\n- Are the held-constant environment pin choices (float64, CPU, single-thread primary + 4-thread sensitivity, identical solver tolerance) acceptable?\\n- Is the fairness standard for `create_graph=True` baselines \\\"first-idea documented usage\\\" or \\\"best-practices\\\" (per item 7)?\\n- Is recording the time difference as secondary and the stability difference as primary acceptable, given the CPU-only environment (per item 5)?\\n- Anything in \\u00a73 you want reframed, especially the answer to your flagged question in \\u00a73 item 1?\", \"evidence\": \"Repository inspection, executed inside /workspace and across the filesystem:\\n- ls -la /workspace -> one entry only: -rw------- ... 521 ... task.json (no subdirectories).\\n- find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -> only task.json.\\n- find / -iname '*torchopt*' (case-insensitive, maxdepth 5) -> zero results across the entire filesystem.\\n- find / -name '*.md' and -iname 'README*', excluding /usr/share/doc, /usr/lib, /proc, /sys -> unrelated system files only; no TorchOpt docs or README.\\n- ls /workspace/track.md -> \\\"No such file or directory\\\"; track.md referenced inside task.json is absent.\\n- python3 -c \\\"import sys; print(sys.version)\\\" -> 3.12.3.\\n- python3 -c \\\"import torch\\\" -> ModuleNotFoundError.\\n- python3 -c \\\"import torchopt\\\" -> ModuleNotFoundError.\\n- python3 -c \\\"import numpy\\\" -> ModuleNotFoundError.\\n- nproc -> 24; /proc/cpuinfo -> 24 logical processors.\\n- /home/actor and /tmp are empty; /root does not exist.\\n- task.json self-declares \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n- Environment summary: runtime=bubblewrap, network=disabled, gpu_enabled=false.\\nExplicitly NOT done: no TorchOpt source was inspected (none present); no TorchOpt API names were quoted from local files; no installation attempt was made (network disabled, no preinstalled deps).\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-01069276-a8e6-4875-90a7-2565e31b9551", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 41}
