{"id": "6db255c1-f738-430a-b0e7-096f5220fa2b", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T19:00:39.962156", "source": "user", "parent_id": "5da3e4ef-ae3a-4074-a008-ebc436879de2", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Подход одобряю, но с конкретными решениями по всем открытым пунктам — фиксируй их явно в финальном дизайне как binding assumptions, не как рекомендации. По пунктам: (1) Объём — T3 (CNN) выкидываем, оставляем T1 (ridge, выпуклая) + T2 (2-слой MLP, невыпуклая). CNN-секция вписывается только если в процессе написания выяснится, что стабильностная ось остаётся недоопределённой; иначе не пишем. Кросс-валидация по задачам (T1×T2) × K(10/100/1000) уже даёт нужную факторизацию. (2) Имена API — все кандидаты из §2.2 (`torchopt.diff.implicit.grad`, `MetaOptimizer`, `torchopt.diff.functional`) оставляем, но в финальный дизайн ОБЯЗАТЕЛЬНО добавляешь явный преамбульный раздел 'Source-verification gate' длиной в 2-3 абзаца, в котором честно прописываешь: (а) в данной среде TorchOpt недоступен, имена приведены по публичной документации и должны быть подтверждены против checkout'а репозитория перед исполнением; (б) если имена/сигнатуры не совпадут, дизайн переписывается, а не переименовывается в 'тот же API'. Имена в коде псевдопримеров помечай маркером `[verify]`. (3) Зафиксированное окружение — принимаю всё как в §2.5: float64 везде, CPU only (GPU отключён, `torch.cuda.Event` недоступен, об этом пишешь явно как об environmental ceiling), `OMP/MKL_NUM_THREADS=1` + `set_num_threads(1)` как primary, повтор на `set_num_threads(4)` как единственная thread-sensitivity проба, `taskset -c 0-3` для подавления scheduler-шума, manifest версий. Никаких float32-ран в этом дизайне. (4) Fairness для `create_graph=True` — стандартный documented form, без `allow_unused` трюков, без `torch.compile`, без ручного checkpointing; это baseline результат. Best-practices-вариант (с `torch.compile` для fair-честного сравнения на современном стеке) упоминаешь в разделе 'Limitations and Threats to Validity' одной строкой как путь для исполнения на GPU-среде. (5) Иерархия метрик — обе первичные (`computation time` и `numerical stability`), но в CPU-only среде акцент делаешь на stability + algorithmic-flops, а time помечаешь как secondary с явной preamble о том, что CUDA-оптимизации TorchOpt (kernel fusion, graph caching) на CPU не измеряются. Это не баг дизайна, это честное ограничение среды. (6) Мой критический вопрос из §3 п.1 — дизайн уже корректно изолирует два эффекта: Treatment A (implicit-diff, полный hypergrad через reuse) vs Treatment B (MetaOptimizer-style inner-state reuse без implicit-diff) vs C2 (unrolled `create_graph=True`, без reuse). В финальном дизайне это преподносишь как основную ablation-таблицу. Если источник подтвердит, что API не даёт отдельный путь для этих двух эффектов — дизайн упрощаешь до A vs C2 и B vs C2 как двух отдельных сравнений. Теперь пиши полный финальный experiment design со следующей структурой (обязательные секции, без пропусков): (i) Research question и гипотезы (H1: время ниже при reuse-of-intermediates при K≥100; H2: stability proxy лучше у implicit-diff при K≥100 на T1; явно отмечаешь, что направление и величина — ожидания, не гарантии); (ii) Rationale — почему именно эти задачи/контролы/метрики отвечают на вопрос проекта; (iii) Treatments & controls — таблица всех A/B/C1/C2/C3/C4 с кратким описанием каждого; (iv) Environment и held constants — выносишь в отдельную секцию, не смешиваешь с методами; (v) Measurement protocol — пошагово: warm-up, число реплик R≥30, фиксация seed, формулы для stability proxy (отдельно для T1 и T2, аналитический reference vs finite-difference), с явным указанием, что exact API-имена подтверждаются на этапе source-verification gate; (vi) Statistical analysis — Wilcoxon signed-rank paired по seed для каждой пары, поправка на множественность (Holm или Bonferroni), reporting median + IQR + p95, заранее заданные effect-size пороги; (vii) Feasibility — одна страница с тем, что РЕАЛЬНО потребуется для исполнения (Python 3.10+, pip install torch torchopt numpy scipy в GPU-среде с CUDA, ориентировочное время на одну ячейку grid, минимальный и амбициозный scope); (viii) Threat-to-validity — CPU-only, BLAS-зависимость, scheduler-шум, semantic drift API, отсутствие эталонного CPU бенчмарка TorchOpt; (ix) Source-verification gate — отдельный абзац перед (iii), как договорились; (x) Limitations & open questions. Никаких выдуманных результатов, никаких псевдо-output'ов, никаких 'ожидаемый график показывает X'. Стоп строго после секции (x). Пиши.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 43}
{"id": "8bf67d99-ab69-4bb2-9ef8-7d2d45288bd7", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T19:02:49.593981", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "6db255c1-f738-430a-b0e7-096f5220fa2b", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-2bbe4ddf-6228-4c46-832f-28a6bcfc6435", "tool_call": {"id": "call-2bbe4ddf-6228-4c46-832f-28a6bcfc6435", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Final experiment design with binding decisions applied\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"# Final Experiment Design — TorchOpt explicit gradient-differentiation API vs standard PyTorch autograd\\n\\nDesign-only specification. No experiment is executed in this environment; no results, no pseudo-outputs, no projected plots are claimed.\\n\\nBinding decisions from the researcher review (recorded up front, not as recommendations):\\n- D1 Scope: Tasks T1 (ridge, convex) + T2 (2-layer MLP, non-convex). T3 (CNN) is excluded and will appear in the design only if, during writing, the stability axis is found underdetermined — and not otherwise.\\n- D2 API surface: candidate names `torchopt.diff.implicit.grad`, `torchopt.MetaOptimizer`, `torchopt.diff.functional` are retained as reference names. They MUST be confirmed against an actual TorchOpt source checkout before execution; pseudo-example invocations are tagged `[verify]`. If the names/signatures do not match the installed source, the design is rewritten, not relabelled.\\n- D3 Environment pinning: float64 everywhere, CPU only, `torch.cuda.Event` unavailable, `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1` + `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1` + `torch.set_num_threads(1)` as primary, repeat at `set_num_threads(4)` as the sole thread-sensitivity probe, `taskset -c 0-3` for CPU affinity, software-version manifest required. No float32 runs.\\n- D4 Fairness for `create_graph=True`: standard, documented form with no `allow_unused`, no `torch.compile`, no manual activation checkpointing. Best-practices variant is acknowledged in §(x) Limitations in a single line as a future path on a GPU host.\\n- D5 Primary metrics: both time and stability are primary in general; ON THIS CPU-ONLY HOST, stability + algorithmic-flop accounting is the primary evidentiary axis, time is secondary, with an explicit preamble that CUDA-leaning optimisations in TorchOpt (kernel fusion, graph caching) are not in scope of this measurement. This is an honest environmental ceiling, not a design bug.\\n- D6 Reuse-claim isolation: the design treats Treatment A (implicit-diff hypergradient, full reuse path) vs Treatment B (MetaOptimizer-style inner-state reuse, no implicit-diff) vs C2 (unrolled `create_graph=True`, no reuse) as a 3-way ablation. If the source-verification gate finds that TorchOpt does not expose two distinct paths for these effects, the design simplifies to two independent comparisons (A vs C2 and B vs C2) without changing the held-constant environment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (i) Research question and hypotheses\\n\\nResearch question. Will TorchOpt's explicit gradient-differentiation API — characterised by reuse of intermediate data and minimisation of memory allocation — reduce computation time and improve numerical stability relative to standard PyTorch autograd implementations?\\n\\nHypotheses (a priori expectations, NOT guarantees; direction and magnitude are conjectural and explicitly not pre-quoted):\\n\\n- H1 (computation time, secondary on this CPU host per D5). On T2 with `K ≥ 100`, the wall-clock per inner solve under TorchOpt's explicit/differentiable optimisation pathway (Treatments A and/or B) is lower than under unrolled `create_graph=True` autograd (C2). Hypothesised direction: lower. Hypothesised magnitude: order-of-K scaling — flat or sub-linear for `Treatment A` vs roughly linear with K for `C2` — but no specific factor is claimed. On T1, the hypothesised magnitude is small (linear inner problem).\\n- H2 (numerical stability, primary on this CPU host per D5). On T1 with `K ≥ 100`, the relative-error stability proxy of Treatment A is closer to the analytical reference than that of C2. On T2 (no analytical reference), the same proxy against a high-precision implicit-diff / finite-difference reference follows the same expected direction. Direction expected: lower `rel_err`. Magnitude: not pre-specified.\\n\\nBoth H1 and H2 are stated as expectations; this design will provide the conditions under which they can be tested, not the outcome.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (ii) Rationale\\n\\nWhy these tasks. T1 (synthetic Gaussian-design ridge regression, scalar outer variable `λ`) gives a closed-form hypergradient, so the stability claim can be tested against an exact reference — this is the cleanest test of the numerical-stability axis. T2 (2-layer MLP on synthetic classification, scalar outer variable `α`) exercises non-convex conditioning where unrolled-differentiation methods degrade as K grows; this is the regime where the reuse-of-intermediates claim is most load-bearing. (T1 × T2) is the minimum factorisation that spans both convex and non-convex inner losses. The K-sweep `{10, 100, 1000}` spans one decade below and one above the typical MAML inner-loop length and is the primary speed/stability axis.\\n\\nWhy these controls. C2 (`create_graph=True` autograd, standard documented form) is the most informative fair-naive comparator for an unrolled inner loop. C1 (vanilla `loss.backward()`, no graph) is the practitioner default. C3 (`torch.func` / `functorch.jacrev`) is the modern JAX-style alternative and is the most likely competitor on speed. C4 (hand-rolled implicit-diff, `J^T J v = g` via CG at fixed tolerance) is a numerical-stability reference, not a speed baseline.\\n\\nWhy these two primary metrics. Wall-clock per inner solve gives a direct measurement of the time claim. The relative-error stability proxy against an analytical reference (T1) or a numerical reference (T2 — central differences at step `h ≈ 1e-5` in float64) gives a direct measurement of the stability claim. The implicit-system matrix condition number `κ(A)` is a secondary stability signal that is meaningful only for implicit-diff paths. Outer-loss variance across `R` seeds is reported as a coarse but robust secondary stability indicator.\\n\\nWhy a held-constant environment. Pinning threads / BLAS / seed makes timing differences attributable to algorithmic choice, not scheduler contention. Restricting to float64 isolates numerical-stability signals from float32 round-off — the only honest precision regime in which to evaluate the stability claim.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (ix) Source-verification gate\\n\\nThis gate executes before any other section of §(iii) and below is treated as executable. It produces a go / rewrite decision; the design does not proceed past it with non-matching API names.\\n\\n(a) Local environment status, recorded factually. TorchOpt is not present in this workspace: `/workspace` contains only `task.json`. A filesystem-wide search for any directory or file matching `*torchopt*` returns zero hits on this bubblewrap host. `python3` is 3.12.3; `import torch` and `import torchopt` both raise `ModuleNotFoundError`. The runtime is bubblewrap with the network disabled. Consequently, no local verification of TorchOpt API names, function signatures, default arguments, or behaviour is possible in this environment. The candidate surface (`torchopt.diff.implicit.grad`, `torchopt.MetaOptimizer`, `torchopt.diff.functional`) is drawn from public TorchOpt documentation and is NOT confirmed against an installed source. Every invocation in this design is therefore marked `[verify]` — a marker indicating \\\"this code is the documented intention; replacement is required if source-confirmation finds a disagreement.\\\"\\n\\n(b) Verification procedure when execution becomes feasible. (1) Acquire a TorchOpt source checkout matching the target Python and PyTorch versions; record the commit SHA in the manifest from §(iv). (2) For each `[verify]` invocation site, locate the corresponding function or method in the source. (3) Verify that the module path, function name, signature, default solver tolerance, default iteration cap, and the inner-state reuse semantics match the description in §(iii). (4) Record the verified name, signature, and any default-tolerance value in an appendix of the experiment report. (5) If any verification step fails, the design is rewritten to use the actual surface — names are not relabelled to preserve the appearance of having used the intended API. Specifically: if the source shows only ONE of the two intended differentiable paths (i.e., TorchOpt does not separately expose \\\"implicit-diff reuse\\\" and \\\"MetaOptimizer-state reuse\\\"), the design simplifies per D6 to two isolated comparisons (Treatment A vs C2; Treatment B vs C2) rather than fabricating a separation.\\n\\nThe Source-verification gate occupies one working day in the feasibility estimate of §(vii). No §(iii)–§(x) section of this design is implemented before the gate passes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (iii) Treatments and controls\\n\\n| ID | Method | Description | Role | Reuse axis |\\n|----|--------|-------------|------|----------------|\\n| A | TorchOpt implicit-gradient path | `g_outer = torchopt.diff.implicit.grad(loss_inner, outer_var, ...)[verify]` returning a hypergradient computed via an implicit-differentiation fixed-point / linear solve; inner intermediates are reused rather than unrolled. | Treatment (implicit-diff reuse) | Full (intermediates + hypergrad through reuse) |\\n| B | TorchOpt MetaOptimizer path | `meta_opt = torchopt.MetaOptimizer(net, inner_opt, ...)[verify]` wrapping a persistent inner optimiser whose state (e.g., momentum / Adam second moment) survives across the K inner steps; outer hypergradient via `torch.autograd.grad` on the meta-update. | Treatment (inner-state reuse, no implicit-diff) | State reuse only |\\n| C1 | Vanilla autograd | `loss.backward()` K times, no graph retention, hypergradient derived from the final inner state by hand. | Baseline — practitioner default | None |\\n| C2 | Unrolled autograd `create_graph=True` | `loss.backward(create_graph=True)` repeated K times, then `torch.autograd.grad` on the outer loss. Standard documented form: no `allow_unused`, no `torch.compile`, no manual checkpointing. | Baseline — fair naive unrolled comparator | None |\\n| C3 | `torch.func` / `functorch.jacrev` | Modern JAX-style reverse-mode AD on the inner-loop functional form. | Baseline — modern alternative | Library-dependent |\\n| C4 | Hand-rolled implicit-diff reference | Solve the inner-loop implicit system `(I − ∂step/∂w) v = g` directly via CG at a fixed tight tolerance. | Reference — stability ceiling | None (direct solve) |\\n\\nPrimary ablation table (per D6): A vs C2 (tests the implicit-diff reuse claim in isolation), B vs C2 (tests the inner-state reuse claim in isolation), with C1 and C3 as secondary contextual comparators and C4 as the stability reference.\\n\\nPseudocode flavour (every TorchOpt call is `[verify]`-tagged):\\n\\n```python\\n# T1 (ridge, scalar outer lambda), per-method header\\ndef make_method(name, inner_lr, K, lambda_init):\\n    if name == \\\"A\\\":\\n        def hypergrad(w0, X, y, lam):\\n            loss_inner = lambda w: ((X @ w - y)**2).mean() + lam * (w**2).mean()\\n            return torchopt.diff.implicit.grad(  # [verify]\\n                loss_inner, lam, wrt=lam, argnums=(0,)\\n            )(w0)[0]\\n        return hypergrad\\n    elif name == \\\"B\\\":\\n        meta_opt = torchopt.MetaOptimizer(  # [verify]\\n            torchopt.MetaSGD(net, inner_lr)[verify],  # [verify]\\n            warp=True,\\n        )[verify]\\n        ...\\n    elif name == \\\"C2\\\":\\n        def hypergrad(w0, X, y, lam):\\n            w = w0.detach().requires_grad_(True)\\n            for _ in range(K):\\n                loss = ((X @ w - X @ y)**2).mean() + lam * (w**2).mean()\\n                grad = torch.autograd.grad(loss, w, create_graph=True)[0]\\n                w = (w - inner_lr * grad).detach().requires_grad_(True)\\n            # final-state hypergradient by-hand from the terminal w\\n            ...\\n        return hypergrad\\n    # ... C1, C3, C4 similarly\\n```\\n\\nA fallback simplification is recorded: under the conditional in §(ix)(b), if the actual TorchOpt surface exposes only one of {A, B}, the table collapses to two independent comparisons (A vs C2) and (B vs C2) without other design changes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (iv) Environment and held constants\\n\\nThis section is everything held constant across cells; mixing these with methods in §(iii) was deliberately avoided.\\n\\n- dtype: float64 throughout. No float32 runs.\\n- device: CPU only — GPU is disabled in the host. `torch.cuda.Event` is unavailable; using `time.perf_counter_ns` instead (see §(v)).\\n- threads (primary): `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1`, `torch.set_num_threads(1)`.\\n- threads (sensitivity probe only): repeat the main result at `torch.set_num_threads(4)`; this is the single axis of thread-sensitivity exploration, run as a separate post-hoc check.\\n- CPU affinity: `taskset -c 0-3` to suppress scheduler noise on the 24-logical-CPU host.\\n- BLAS: the BLAS that ships with the PyTorch CPU build (OpenBLAS or MKL); recorded once in the manifest and not swapped mid-run.\\n- software manifest (required artifact): exact versions of `python`, `torch`, `torchopt`, `numpy`, `scipy`, plus the TorchOpt source `git rev-parse HEAD` recorded by the Source-verification gate (D2); the BLAS implementation and version; the Linux kernel version; the `taskset` and environment-variable values logged at run start.\\n- seeds: one master seed; per replicate `seed_r = master_seed XOR r`; same `seed_r` used identically across all methods in that replicate (this enables the paired statistics in §(vi)).\\n- inner-optimizer hyperparameters: held constant within each task across methods.\\n- inner step K: held constant per cell across methods.\\n- outer (hyper) optimizer: plain SGD at a fixed step size, identical across methods so any time difference is attributable to inner-loop differentiation, not outer-loop internals.\\n- inner loss reduction: mean.\\n- implicit-diff solver: identical default tolerance and iteration cap across Treatments A, A-conditional B-equivalent, and C4.\\n- dataset: synthetic, generated once per (task, K, seed_r) cell and reused across methods.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (v) Measurement protocol\\n\\nStep-by-step, executed PER CELL (one method × one task × one K):\\n\\n1. Acquire the manifest-validated API per §(ix). If verification has not passed, stop and do not run cells.\\n2. Set environment variables, BLAS threads, `set_num_threads(1)`, `taskset -c 0-3`, dtype (`torch.set_default_dtype(torch.float64)`).\\n3. Generate the synthetic dataset `D_r` for `(task, K, seed_r)`.\\n4. Initialise inner and outer parameters to a deterministic function of `seed_r` (identical across methods).\\n5. Warm-up: run 5 full inner-solve-roundtrips without timing, to amortise one-time allocations and BLAS dispatch.\\n6. Timed run: `R ≥ 30` rounds. Per round:\\n   a. Reset torch / numpy random state from `seed_r`.\\n   b. `t0 = time.perf_counter_ns()`.\\n   c. Execute K inner optimiser steps under the method's chosen differentiation mode.\\n   d. Compute the hypergradient w.r.t. the outer variable.\\n   e. Apply one outer SGD update step.\\n   f. `t1 = time.perf_counter_ns()`; record `dt = (t1 - t0) / 1e6` in ms.\\n7. Per round, also record:\\n   - `g_method_r`: the hypergradient vector.\\n   - `L_inner_terminal_r`: the inner loss at step K.\\n   - For implicit-diff methods only (`A` and `C4`): the implicit-system residual `‖A v − g‖₂` and the matrix condition number `κ(A)` (if exposed by the solver; otherwise computed via `numpy.linalg.cond(A)` on the assembled matrix for the post-hoc `C4` reference).\\n8. Compute stability proxies from the `g_method_r` series.\\n\\nStability proxy — Task T1 (convex ridge, analytical reference):\\n\\nFor ridge regression with normal-equation inner solution `w*(λ) = (XᵀX + λ I)⁻¹ Xᵀy`, the hypergradient `g_ref(λ) = d/dλ L_outer(w*(λ))` is computable analytically by differentiating the closed-form path. The stability proxy is:\\n\\n    rel_err_T1(method, r) = ‖g_method_r − g_ref(λ)‖₂ / ‖g_ref(λ)‖₂\\n\\nwith `g_ref` recomputed on the same `D_r` and the same `λ` per replicate. For the implicit-diff methods the residual `‖A v − g‖₂` at termination is reported per round alongside the rel_err:\\n\\n    impl_residual_T1(method, r) = ‖A(λ_r) v_r − g_r‖₂\\n\\nStability proxy — Task T2 (non-convex MLP, finite-difference reference):\\n\\nNo analytical reference is available. Use central finite differences in float64 at step `h ≈ 1e-5` on α (with Richardson extrapolation optionally):\\n\\n    g_ref_fd(α_r) = (L_outer(α_r + h) − L_outer(α_r − h)) / (2h)\\n\\nThe stability proxy is:\\n\\n    rel_err_T2(method, r) = ‖g_method_r − g_ref_fd(α_r)‖₂ / ‖g_ref_fd(α_r)‖₂\\n\\nThe finite-difference reference cost is negligible compared to inner-solve cost at K ≥ 100 and is paid once per replicate per task; round-level timing is applied only to the method's own inner solve, not the reference.\\n\\nSecondary stability signals:\\n\\n    cond_Tx(method, r) = κ(A_r)        # implicit-diff paths only\\n    outer_loss_var(method) = Var_{r}[L_inner_terminal_r]\\n\\nMemory proxy: `tracemalloc` peak around one inner solve per cell. Labelled explicitly as proxy, not primary.\\n\\nPer-cell artefacts recorded: `dt_r` series (length R), `g_method_r` series, `g_ref` series, `impl_residual_*` series (implicit-diff only), `cond_*` series (implicit-diff only), `L_inner_terminal_r` series, `tracemalloc` peak. All written in a single per-cell HDF5 / JSON artefact under `(task, K, method, seed_master)`.\\n\\nThe exact TorchOpt API names used (e.g., for `A`, the function formerly or currently named `torchopt.diff.implicit.grad`[verify], and the solver argument list [verify]) are NOT locked until the Source-verification gate (§ix) passes and the verified names are appended to the experiment report.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (vi) Statistical analysis\\n\\nUnit of analysis: one replicate `r ∈ {1 .. R}` paired across methods by `seed_r` (and hence by `D_r` and initial-state seed).\\n\\nPrimary statistic. Wilcoxon signed-rank test, paired by `seed_r`, applied to the per-replicate observation under comparison. Applied to:\\n- the timing vector `dt_r(method=baseline) − dt_r(method=Treatment)` for each (Treatment × baseline) pair — O2n vs O2n pairs;\\n- the stability vector `rel_err_r(method=baseline) − rel_err_r(method=Treatment)`, aligned in sign to \\\"lower is better\\\" — same pairing.\\n\\nMultiple-comparison correction. Holm correction across the family of comparisons within a (task, K) cell. Number of pairwise comparisons per cell = `|Treatments| × |Baselines|` = `2 × 3 = 6` (Treatments A,B × Baselines C1,C2,C3) — under Holm this is the family controlling FWER at α = 0.05. Bonferroni is recorded as a sensitivity check (more conservative); Holm is the headline.\\n\\nReporting. Per cell:\\n- median and IQR (p25 / p75) of `dt_r` and of `rel_err_r`, separately for each method.\\n- p95 of `dt_r` as a tail-latency indicator.\\n- For each pairwise signed-rank test: the W statistic, Holm-adjusted p-value, and the median signed difference (`Δ̃`) with its 95 % bootstrap CI (B = 2000 resamples).\\n- Pre-specified effect-size threshold: a meaningful difference is one where either (a) the Holm-adjusted p < 0.05 AND | `Δ̃` / max(p75−p25 across the two methods, 1e-12) | ≥ 0.5, OR (b) the Holm-adjusted p ≥ 0.05, in which case the cell is reported as \\\"no detectable difference\\\" (NOT \\\"no effect\\\").\\n- Smaller-than-threshold effects are not claimed as wins or losses under this design.\\n\\nPower. With R = 30 paired replicates and α = 0.05 (two-sided), the Wilcoxon signed-rank test has approximately 80 % power to detect effect sizes around 0.5 SD in the paired differences; this is the source of the |Δ̃ / IQR| ≥ 0.5 pre-specified threshold above. 15 %-level speed-ups are detectable in well-behaved cells; smaller speed-ups will be reported as \\\"no detectable difference\\\".\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (vii) Feasibility — what execution actually needs\\n\\nRuntime environment required (NOT available in the current host):\\n- Python ≥ 3.10 (the current host has 3.12.3, which is fine).\\n- `pip install torch torchopt numpy scipy` performed in a GPU-enabled environment with a CUDA build of PyTorch. The current host is bubblewrap with the network disabled and no PyTorch / TorchOpt preinstalled — it is not the right host for execution. A typical TorchOpt install path is `pip install torch torchopt`, with the GPU-CUDA build of `torch` targeted to the host CUDA driver.\\n- The Source-verification gate requires a TorchOpt source checkout (not just the wheel) so that the exact API names under import can be inspected; record the `git rev-parse HEAD`.\\n\\nCompute budget per cell (rough, to be calibrated at execution):\\n- T1 with K = 10, K = 100: a few seconds per replicate per method on one CPU core; with R = 30, one cell ≈ a few minutes.\\n- T1 with K = 1000: tens of seconds per replicate per method; cell ≈ tens of minutes.\\n- T2 with K = 10: seconds per replicate per method; T2 with K = 1000 dominates the budget at tens of minutes to hours per replicate. Cells under C2 (`create_graph=True`, full unroll) are the slowest.\\n- With methods × (T1, T2) × {10, 100, 1000} × R = 30 × 6 = 36 cells, total wall-clock is dominated by T2 × K = 1000 × C2 and the thread-sensitivity repeat (multiplier ≤ 2). Indicative order: low double-digit CPU-hours on a single pinned core, multiplies with any additional sensitivity sweeps.\\n\\nMinimal scope (priority 1, BUDGET-CONSTRAINED execution path): T1 only, K ∈ {100, 1000}, all six methods, R = 30, no thread-sensitivity sweep, no tolerance sweep. Tests H1 and H2 along the convex-stability axis. Estimated wall-clock: low single-digit CPU-hours.\\n\\nAmbitious scope (full design as specified): T1 + T2 × K ∈ {10, 100, 1000} × all six methods × R = 30 + thread-sensitivity (1 axis × 1 repeat) + tolerance sweep (3 values) × R = 30. Estimated wall-clock: low-to-mid double-digit CPU-hours.\\n\\nArtefacts. A version manifest; a Source-verification appendix; per-cell HDF5/JSON artefacts; a per-cell statistics table; an aggregate cross-cell table with Holm-adjusted p-values; an ablation table for the A vs C2 and B vs C2 comparisons.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (viii) Threats to validity\\n\\n- Environmental ceiling: CPU-only host. CUDA-leaning TorchOpt optimisations (kernel fusion, autograd-graph caching, compiled-graph reuse) are NOT in scope of measurement on this host. The time axis therefore reflects algorithmic-flop accounting and Python/CPU dispatch cost rather than end-to-end GPU performance. The stability axis is independent of GPU availability and remains a primary evidentiary signal here.\\n- BLAS dependency: the exact BLAS implementation (OpenBLAS vs MKL) is a co-variate with timing and, weakly, with numerical stability. Recorded in the manifest; not swept within the design because sweeping BLAS confounds the method comparison.\\n- Scheduler / thermal noise: `taskset -c 0-3`, `set_num_threads(1)`, `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1` mitigate but do not eliminate residual noise. Cells with median-p95 spread > 2× are reported with that caveat and not aggregated into headline comparisons without the thread-sensitivity check.\\n- Semantic drift of the TorchOpt API across versions: the Source-verification gate (§ix) is the only mitigation. If the gate finds a non-matching surface, the design pivots; it does not relabel.\\n- Absence of a canonical CPU benchmark for TorchOpt: there is no published baseline of TorchOpt performance on CPU. This means the magnitude claims in H1 / H2 cannot be anchored against prior literature for this host; the experimental design generates fresh data without that anchor.\\n- Fairness of `create_graph=True` documented form: held by D4 to the standard form with no `allow_unused`, no `torch.compile`, no manual checkpointing. This may not represent practitioner best-practices; §(x) Limitations notes the best-practices variant as a single-line future-path on a GPU host.\\n- Synthetic-data external validity: T1 and T2 use synthetic data so the conditioning axis is controllable, but the conclusions may not transfer to real datasets. Documented as a limitation rather than a threat-at-the-design-level.\\n- Single-task inner loop: a multi-task (MAML-style task averaging) inner loop is intentionally excluded (D1) to avoid confounding the reuse-claim test with task-averaging dynamics. Recorded here as a validity scope-limit, not a threat to the per-task conclusions.\\n- Scalar outer hyperparameter: the outer hyperparameter is scalar in both T1 and T2. Vector-valued outer hyperparameter would change the implicit-diff linear-system conditioning profile. Recorded here as a scope limit.\\n- Effect-size threshold calibration: the |Δ̃ / IQR| ≥ 0.5 threshold is reasonable for R = 30 but is not a literature-standard for hypergradient comparisons; a more orthodox report adds Cohen's d (paired) and Cliff's δ, both of which are derivable from the recorded per-cell data without re-execution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (x) Limitations and open questions\\n\\n- Best-practices comparator, GPU path. As a single-line acknowledgement of D4: a `create_graph=True + torch.compile` baseline on a GPU host is the practitioner-best-practices comparator. It is not in this design's scope and is recorded only as the natural next step on a CUDA-equipped execution host.\\n- Multi-task inner loop. Whether the A/B/C2 separation of effects reproduces under task averaging (MAML-style) is open. The design's scope-limit is single-task; an extension would add a multi-task cell at K = 5 inner steps (matching canonical few-shot setup) on a single representative task distribution, but only as an additional experiment.\\n- Vector outer hyperparameter. Open question: does the implicit-diff reuse advantage persist (or grow) when the outer hyperparameter is vector-valued (multiple regularizer strengths or per-layer learning rates)? The current design restricts the outer hyperparameter to scalar in both tasks to keep the analytical reference for T1 and the FD reference for T2 tractable.\\n- K range extrapolation. The K-sweep {10, 100, 1000} spans the MAML-relevant inner-loop range; extrapolation beyond K = 1000 is unverified.\\n- Source drift rebuild cadence. The Source-verification gate runs once per execution; if TorchOpt revises the implicit-diff API between the gate and the run, the design should re-gate.\\n- Hypergradient reference for T2. Central finite differences at h ≈ 1e-5 in float64 are the chosen reference for T2; Richardson extrapolation is permissible as a sensitivity check but not as the primary reference. The `O(h²)` truncation error of central differences is a small known bias in `g_ref_fd` that translates to a known floor in `rel_err_T2`. Cells whose `rel_err_T2` is comparable to or below this floor are reported as \\\"below FD precision floor\\\" rather than as \\\"no error\\\".\\n- Outer-loss variance. Reported as a secondary stability signal across seeds; pre-registered not as a primary.\\n- Memory proxy specificity. `tracemalloc` is a Python-allocation-level proxy, not a tensor-level memory profile. On a CPU host this is the best available proxy short of intercepting PyTorch's allocator; it is reported as such.\\n- No execution in this environment. Per the environment ceiling and D5: this is a design-only artefact. No experiment was run; no per-cell artefacts were produced; no plots are claimed.\\n\\n— end of design —\",\"evidence\":\"Designer-recorded facts (no claim is made beyond what these ground):\\n- Repository paths and contents: `/workspace/task.json` is the only file in `/workspace`; `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` returns only that file.\\n- TorchOpt presence on the host: `find / -iname '*torchopt*'` returns zero results; no TorchOpt source or wheel is anywhere on the filesystem.\\n- Python and module status: `python3 -V` -> 3.12.3; `import torch` -> ModuleNotFoundError; `import torchopt` -> ModuleNotFoundError; `import numpy` -> ModuleNotFoundError. No project dependency is installed.\\n- task.json self-declares \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n- Environment summary from the wrapper context: bubblewrap runtime, network disabled, gpu_enabled=false, cpu_affinity_count=24.\\n- track.md is referenced inside task.json's documents field but does not exist on disk.\\n- No TorchOpt API names, function signatures, default tolerance values, or quoted paper claims were taken from local source; every TorchOpt-named call in pseudo-example code carries an explicit `[verify]` marker indicating that the name is documented-intention only and must be confirmed against an installed source checkout before execution.\\n\\nBinding decisions applied verbatim from the researcher's review message:\\n- D1 scope: T1 (ridge) + T2 (2-layer MLP); T3 (CNN) dropped by default, included only if the stability axis becomes underdetermined during writing; this did not occur, so T3 is not written into the design.\\n- D2 API surface: retained candidates with a Source-verification gate placed before section (iii) covering both (a) unavailability and (b) the rewrite-not-relabel rule; pseudo-example code carries `[verify]` markers.\\n- D3 environment: float64 only, CPU only, `omp/mkl_num_threads=1`, `set_num_threads(1)` primary, `set_num_threads(4)` sensitivity probe, `taskset -c 0-3`, manifest required, CUDA unavailable recorded.\\n- D4 fairness for `create_graph=True`: standard documented form; best-practices variant (with `torch.compile`) is a single-line acknowledgement in §(x) for a GPU host.\\n- D5 metrics hierarchy: both primary in general; on this CPU host, stability + algorithmic flops is primary and time is secondary with an explicit preamble that CUDA TorchOpt optimisations are out of scope.\\n- D6 reuse-claim isolation: A vs B vs C2 is the primary 3-way ablation; with a fallback simplification to two independent (A vs C2) and (B vs C2) comparisons if the source-verification gate finds TorchOpt does not expose two distinct paths.\\n\\nNo execution was performed; no results, no pseudocode-output, no projected plot was claimed anywhere in this design.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"R = 30 paired replicates is the minimum execution budget; no claim of higher statistical power is made.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Effect-size threshold |Δ̃ / max(IQR, 1e-12)| ≥ 0.5 with Holm-adjusted p < 0.05 is the pre-specified bar for \\\"meaningful difference\\\"; non-significant cells are reported as \\\"no detectable difference\\\".</item>\"},\"$text\":\"H1 and H2 are stated as a-priori directional expectations only; no specific magnitude, factor, or numerical target is claimed anywhere in the design.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D6 reuse-claim isolation: the design presents A vs B vs C2 as a 3-way ablation; if the source-verification gate finds TorchOpt exposes only one differentiable path, the design simplifies to (A vs C2) and (B vs C2) as two independent comparisons without other changes.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D5 metric hierarchy: both metrics are primary in general; on this CPU host stability plus algorithmic-flop accounting is the primary evidentiary axis and time is secondary, with the explicit acknowledgement that CUDA TorchOpt optimisations are out of scope.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D4 fairness: `create_graph=True` baseline uses the standard documented form without `allow_unused`, `torch.compile`, or manual checkpointing; the best-practices variant is acknowledged only in §(x) as a future GPU-host path.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D3 environment: float64 throughout; CPU only; `torch.cuda.Event` is unavailable; threads pinned to 1 in the primary run with a single 4-thread sensitivity repeat; CPU affinity pinned to cores 0–3; software manifest is a required artefact.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D2 API names: every TorchOpt invocation in pseudo-example code is marked `[verify]`; the design does not proceed past the Source-verification gate in §(ix) unless API names are confirmed against an actual source checkout.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D1 scope: design executes T1 + T2 only; T3 (CNN) is not present in the design and is recorded as drop-default per the binding decision.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Whether the stability axis would have remained underdetermined if T3 had been included in the design — the design records the conditional but, per D1, did not trigger it during writing.</item>\",\"$text\":\"What the implicit-system matrix condition number `κ(A)` actually looks like at K ∈ {100, 1000} for T1 and T2 — no claim is made in absence of data.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the |Δ̃ / IQR| ≥ 0.5 effect-size bar is the right threshold for hypergradient comparisons; alternative thresholds (e.g., paired Cohen's d) may classify the same cells differently.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether `OMP/MKL_NUM_THREADS=1` + `set_num_threads(1)` + `taskset -c 0-3` is sufficient to suppress scheduler noise to the level required for ~15 % effect detection.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the finite-difference reference on T2 has a precision floor that competes with the rel_err observations; cells near the FD precision floor are reported explicitly as such.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the synthetic T1 / T2 datasets are representative enough to generalise to real-data workloads.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the scalar-outer restriction in T1 and T2 limits external validity to scalar hyper-parameter problems; vector-valued outer hyperparameters are out of scope.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the K-sweep {10, 100, 1000} is sufficient to characterise the asymptotic scaling difference between Treatments and C2; extrapolation beyond K = 1000 is unverified by this design.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the TorchOpt version available at execution exposes two distinct differentiable paths (Treatment A implicit-diff reuse and Treatment B MetaOptimizer-state reuse) or only one — the design pivots but does not relabel per §(ix)(b).</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-ce6c81c7-2fdf-4993-aeb9-e495b16758ef", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 44}
{"id": "f995c1fd-8ec2-4f8d-b951-ef558cc92729", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T19:02:49.594916", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "8bf67d99-ab69-4bb2-9ef8-7d2d45288bd7", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-2bbe4ddf-6228-4c46-832f-28a6bcfc6435", "error": "Error validating tool 'end_researcher_turn': 2 validation errors for ReviewHandoffAction\nassumptions.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...nding decision.</item>'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type\nuncertainties.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'... per §(ix)(b).</item>'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type. Parameters provided: ['stage', 'answer', 'evidence', 'assumptions', 'uncertainties']", "classification": {"kind": "agent_action", "retryable": true, "user_action": "retry", "error_id": null}, "kind": "AgentErrorEvent", "source_event_index": 45}
{"id": "1e5c1860-bbbc-4a51-a609-534b565b8a28", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T19:05:00.796185", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "f995c1fd-8ec2-4f8d-b951-ef558cc92729", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-fbf00158-bf8c-474a-be89-2d3ac47109bf", "tool_call": {"id": "call-fbf00158-bf8c-474a-be89-2d3ac47109bf", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Final experiment design with binding decisions applied\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"# Final Experiment Design — TorchOpt explicit gradient-differentiation API vs standard PyTorch autograd\\n\\nDesign-only specification. No experiment is executed in this environment; no results, no pseudo-outputs, no projected plots are claimed.\\n\\nBinding decisions from the researcher review (recorded up front, not as recommendations):\\n- D1 Scope: Tasks T1 (ridge, convex) + T2 (2-layer MLP, non-convex). T3 (CNN) is excluded and will appear in the design only if, during writing, the stability axis is found underdetermined — and not otherwise.\\n- D2 API surface: candidate names `torchopt.diff.implicit.grad`, `torchopt.MetaOptimizer`, `torchopt.diff.functional` are retained as reference names. They MUST be confirmed against an actual TorchOpt source checkout before execution; pseudo-example invocations are tagged `[verify]`. If the names/signatures do not match the installed source, the design is rewritten, not relabelled.\\n- D3 Environment pinning: float64 everywhere, CPU only, `torch.cuda.Event` unavailable, `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1` + `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1` + `torch.set_num_threads(1)` as primary, repeat at `set_num_threads(4)` as the sole thread-sensitivity probe, `taskset -c 0-3` for CPU affinity, software-version manifest required. No float32 runs.\\n- D4 Fairness for `create_graph=True`: standard, documented form with no `allow_unused`, no `torch.compile`, no manual activation checkpointing. Best-practices variant is acknowledged in section (x) Limitations in a single line as a future path on a GPU host.\\n- D5 Primary metrics: both time and stability are primary in general; ON THIS CPU-ONLY HOST, stability + algorithmic-flop accounting is the primary evidentiary axis, time is secondary, with an explicit preamble that CUDA-leaning optimisations in TorchOpt (kernel fusion, graph caching) are not in scope of this measurement. This is an honest environmental ceiling, not a design bug.\\n- D6 Reuse-claim isolation: the design treats Treatment A (implicit-diff hypergradient, full reuse path) vs Treatment B (MetaOptimizer-style inner-state reuse, no implicit-diff) vs C2 (unrolled `create_graph=True`, no reuse) as a 3-way ablation. If the source-verification gate finds that TorchOpt does not expose two distinct paths for these effects, the design simplifies to two independent comparisons (A vs C2) and (B vs C2) without changing the held-constant environment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (i) Research question and hypotheses\\n\\nResearch question. Will TorchOpt's explicit gradient-differentiation API — characterised by reuse of intermediate data and minimisation of memory allocation — reduce computation time and improve numerical stability relative to standard PyTorch autograd implementations?\\n\\nHypotheses (a priori expectations, NOT guarantees; direction and magnitude are conjectural and explicitly not pre-quoted):\\n\\n- H1 (computation time, secondary on this CPU host per D5). On T2 with `K >= 100`, the wall-clock per inner solve under TorchOpt's explicit/differentiable optimisation pathway (Treatments A and/or B) is lower than under unrolled `create_graph=True` autograd (C2). Hypothesised direction: lower. Hypothesised magnitude: order-of-K scaling — flat or sub-linear for Treatment A vs roughly linear with K for C2 — but no specific factor is claimed. On T1, the hypothesised magnitude is small (linear inner problem).\\n- H2 (numerical stability, primary on this CPU host per D5). On T1 with `K >= 100`, the relative-error stability proxy of Treatment A is closer to the analytical reference than that of C2. On T2 (no analytical reference), the same proxy against a high-precision implicit-diff / finite-difference reference follows the same expected direction. Direction expected: lower `rel_err`. Magnitude: not pre-specified.\\n\\nBoth H1 and H2 are stated as expectations; this design will provide the conditions under which they can be tested, not the outcome.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (ii) Rationale\\n\\nWhy these tasks. T1 (synthetic Gaussian-design ridge regression, scalar outer variable lambda) gives a closed-form hypergradient, so the stability claim can be tested against an exact reference — this is the cleanest test of the numerical-stability axis. T2 (2-layer MLP on synthetic classification, scalar outer variable alpha) exercises non-convex conditioning where unrolled-differentiation methods degrade as K grows; this is the regime where the reuse-of-intermediates claim is most load-bearing. (T1 x T2) is the minimum factorisation that spans both convex and non-convex inner losses. The K-sweep {10, 100, 1000} spans one decade below and one above the typical MAML inner-loop length and is the primary speed/stability axis.\\n\\nWhy these controls. C2 (`create_graph=True` autograd, standard documented form) is the most informative fair-naive comparator for an unrolled inner loop. C1 (vanilla `loss.backward()`, no graph) is the practitioner default. C3 (`torch.func` / `functorch.jacrev`) is the modern JAX-style alternative and is the most likely competitor on speed. C4 (hand-rolled implicit-diff, J^T J v = g via CG at fixed tolerance) is a numerical-stability reference, not a speed baseline.\\n\\nWhy these two primary metrics. Wall-clock per inner solve gives a direct measurement of the time claim. The relative-error stability proxy against an analytical reference (T1) or a numerical reference (T2 — central differences at step h approx 1e-5 in float64) gives a direct measurement of the stability claim. The implicit-system matrix condition number kappa(A) is a secondary stability signal that is meaningful only for implicit-diff paths. Outer-loss variance across R seeds is reported as a coarse but robust secondary stability indicator.\\n\\nWhy a held-constant environment. Pinning threads / BLAS / seed makes timing differences attributable to algorithmic choice, not scheduler contention. Restricting to float64 isolates numerical-stability signals from float32 round-off — the only honest precision regime in which to evaluate the stability claim.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (ix) Source-verification gate\\n\\nThis gate executes before any other section of (iii) and below is treated as executable. It produces a go / rewrite decision; the design does not proceed past it with non-matching API names.\\n\\n(a) Local environment status, recorded factually. TorchOpt is not present in this workspace: `/workspace` contains only `task.json`. A filesystem-wide search for any directory or file matching `*torchopt*` returns zero hits on this bubblewrap host. `python3` is 3.12.3; `import torch` and `import torchopt` both raise `ModuleNotFoundError`. The runtime is bubblewrap with the network disabled. Consequently, no local verification of TorchOpt API names, function signatures, default arguments, or behaviour is possible in this environment. The candidate surface (`torchopt.diff.implicit.grad`, `torchopt.MetaOptimizer`, `torchopt.diff.functional`) is drawn from public TorchOpt documentation and is NOT confirmed against an installed source. Every invocation in this design is therefore marked `[verify]` — a marker indicating that the code is the documented intention and replacement is required if source-confirmation finds a disagreement.\\n\\n(b) Verification procedure when execution becomes feasible. (1) Acquire a TorchOpt source checkout matching the target Python and PyTorch versions; record the commit SHA in the manifest from section (iv). (2) For each `[verify]` invocation site, locate the corresponding function or method in the source. (3) Verify that the module path, function name, signature, default solver tolerance, default iteration cap, and the inner-state reuse semantics match the description in (iii). (4) Record the verified name, signature, and any default-tolerance value in an appendix of the experiment report. (5) If any verification step fails, the design is rewritten to use the actual surface — names are not relabelled to preserve the appearance of having used the intended API. Specifically: if the source shows only ONE of the two intended differentiable paths (i.e., TorchOpt does not separately expose \\\"implicit-diff reuse\\\" and \\\"MetaOptimizer-state reuse\\\"), the design simplifies per D6 to two isolated comparisons (Treatment A vs C2; Treatment B vs C2) rather than fabricating a separation.\\n\\nThe Source-verification gate occupies one working day in the feasibility estimate of section (vii). No (iii)–(x) section of this design is implemented before the gate passes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (iii) Treatments and controls\\n\\n- A — TorchOpt implicit-gradient path. `g_outer = torchopt.diff.implicit.grad(loss_inner, outer_var, ...)` [verify], returning a hypergradient computed via an implicit-differentiation fixed-point / linear solve; inner intermediates are reused rather than unrolled. Treatment (implicit-diff reuse). Reuse axis: full (intermediates + hypergrad through reuse).\\n- B — TorchOpt MetaOptimizer path. `meta_opt = torchopt.MetaOptimizer(net, inner_opt, ...)` [verify] wrapping a persistent inner optimiser whose state (e.g., momentum / Adam second moment) survives across the K inner steps; outer hypergradient via `torch.autograd.grad` on the meta-update. Treatment (inner-state reuse, no implicit-diff). Reuse axis: state reuse only.\\n- C1 — Vanilla autograd. `loss.backward()` K times, no graph retention, hypergradient derived from the final inner state by hand. Baseline — practitioner default. Reuse axis: none.\\n- C2 — Unrolled autograd `create_graph=True`. `loss.backward(create_graph=True)` repeated K times, then `torch.autograd.grad` on the outer loss. Standard documented form: no `allow_unused`, no `torch.compile`, no manual checkpointing. Baseline — fair naive unrolled comparator. Reuse axis: none.\\n- C3 — `torch.func` / `functorch.jacrev`. Modern JAX-style reverse-mode AD on the inner-loop functional form. Baseline — modern alternative. Reuse axis: library-dependent.\\n- C4 — Hand-rolled implicit-diff reference. Solve the inner-loop implicit system `(I - d step / d w) v = g` directly via CG at a fixed tight tolerance. Reference — stability ceiling. Reuse axis: none (direct solve).\\n\\nPrimary ablation table (per D6): A vs C2 (tests the implicit-diff reuse claim in isolation), B vs C2 (tests the inner-state reuse claim in isolation), with C1 and C3 as secondary contextual comparators and C4 as the stability reference.\\n\\nPseudocode flavour — every TorchOpt call is `[verify]`-tagged:\\n\\n```python\\n# T1 (ridge, scalar outer lambda), per-method header\\ndef make_method(name, inner_lr, K, lambda_init):\\n    if name == \\\"A\\\":\\n        def hypergrad(w0, X, y, lam):\\n            loss_inner = lambda w: ((X @ w - y) ** 2).mean() + lam * (w ** 2).mean()\\n            # [verify] module path / signature / default solver-tolerance not confirmed in this environment\\n            return torchopt.diff.implicit.grad(loss_inner, lam, wrt=lam, argnums=(0,))(w0)[0]\\n        return hypergrad\\n    elif name == \\\"B\\\":\\n        # [verify] MetaOptimizer surface area only documented-intention\\n        meta_opt = torchopt.MetaOptimizer(torchopt.MetaSGD(net, inner_lr), warp=True)  # all [verify]\\n        ...\\n    elif name == \\\"C2\\\":\\n        def hypergrad(w0, X, y, lam):\\n            w = w0.detach().requires_grad_(True)\\n            for _ in range(K):\\n                loss = ((X @ w - X @ y) ** 2).mean() + lam * (w ** 2).mean()\\n                grad = torch.autograd.grad(loss, w, create_graph=True)[0]\\n                w = (w - inner_lr * grad).detach().requires_grad_(True)\\n            # final-state hypergradient by-hand from the terminal w\\n            ...\\n        return hypergrad\\n    # ... C1, C3, C4 similarly\\n```\\n\\nA fallback simplification is recorded: under the conditional in section (ix)(b), if the actual TorchOpt surface exposes only one of {A, B}, the table collapses to two independent comparisons (A vs C2) and (B vs C2) without other design changes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (iv) Environment and held constants\\n\\nThis section is everything held constant across cells; mixing these with methods in (iii) is deliberately avoided.\\n\\n- dtype: float64 throughout. No float32 runs.\\n- device: CPU only — GPU is disabled in the host. `torch.cuda.Event` is unavailable; `time.perf_counter_ns` is used instead (see (v)).\\n- threads (primary): `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1`, `torch.set_num_threads(1)`.\\n- threads (sensitivity probe only): repeat the main result at `torch.set_num_threads(4)`; this is the single axis of thread-sensitivity exploration, run as a separate post-hoc check.\\n- CPU affinity: `taskset -c 0-3` to suppress scheduler noise on the 24-logical-CPU host.\\n- BLAS: the BLAS that ships with the PyTorch CPU build (OpenBLAS or MKL); recorded once in the manifest and not swapped mid-run.\\n- software manifest (required artifact): exact versions of Python, torch, torchopt, numpy, scipy; TorchOpt source `git rev-parse HEAD` recorded by the Source-verification gate (D2); BLAS implementation and version; Linux kernel version; the `taskset` and environment-variable values logged at run start.\\n- seeds: one master seed; per replicate `seed_r = master_seed XOR r`; same `seed_r` used identically across all methods in that replicate (this enables the paired statistics in (vi)).\\n- inner-optimizer hyperparameters: held constant within each task across methods.\\n- inner step K: held constant per cell across methods.\\n- outer (hyper) optimizer: plain SGD at a fixed step size, identical across methods so any time difference is attributable to inner-loop differentiation, not outer-loop internals.\\n- inner loss reduction: mean.\\n- implicit-diff solver: identical default tolerance and iteration cap across Treatments A, the A-conditional B-equivalent, and C4.\\n- dataset: synthetic, generated once per (task, K, seed_r) cell and reused across methods.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (v) Measurement protocol\\n\\nStep-by-step, executed PER CELL (one method x one task x one K):\\n\\n1. Acquire the manifest-validated API per section (ix). If verification has not passed, stop and do not run cells.\\n2. Set environment variables, BLAS threads, `set_num_threads(1)`, `taskset -c 0-3`, dtype (`torch.set_default_dtype(torch.float64)`).\\n3. Generate the synthetic dataset `D_r` for (task, K, seed_r).\\n4. Initialise inner and outer parameters to a deterministic function of `seed_r` (identical across methods).\\n5. Warm-up: run 5 full inner-solve-roundtrips without timing, to amortise one-time allocations and BLAS dispatch.\\n6. Timed run: `R >= 30` rounds. Per round:\\n   a. Reset torch / numpy random state from `seed_r`.\\n   b. `t0 = time.perf_counter_ns()`.\\n   c. Execute K inner optimiser steps under the method's chosen differentiation mode.\\n   d. Compute the hypergradient w.r.t. the outer variable.\\n   e. Apply one outer SGD update step.\\n   f. `t1 = time.perf_counter_ns()`; record `dt = (t1 - t0) / 1e6` in ms.\\n7. Per round, also record:\\n   - `g_method_r`: the hypergradient vector.\\n   - `L_inner_terminal_r`: the inner loss at step K.\\n   - For implicit-diff methods only (A and C4): the implicit-system residual norm(A v - g) and the matrix condition number kappa(A) (if exposed by the solver; otherwise computed via `numpy.linalg.cond(A)` on the assembled matrix for the post-hoc C4 reference).\\n8. Compute stability proxies from the `g_method_r` series.\\n\\nStability proxy — Task T1 (convex ridge, analytical reference):\\n\\nFor ridge regression with normal-equation inner solution `w*(lambda) = (X^T X + lambda I)^{-1} X^T y`, the hypergradient `g_ref(lambda) = d/d lambda L_outer(w*(lambda))` is computable analytically by differentiating the closed-form path. The stability proxy is:\\n\\n    rel_err_T1(method, r) = || g_method_r - g_ref(lambda) ||_2 / || g_ref(lambda) ||_2\\n\\nwith `g_ref` recomputed on the same `D_r` and the same `lambda` per replicate. For the implicit-diff methods the residual norm(A v - g) at termination is reported per round alongside the rel_err:\\n\\n    impl_residual_T1(method, r) = || A(lambda_r) v_r - g_r ||_2\\n\\nStability proxy — Task T2 (non-convex MLP, finite-difference reference):\\n\\nNo analytical reference is available. Use central finite differences in float64 at step `h` approx 1e-5 on alpha (with Richardson extrapolation optionally):\\n\\n    g_ref_fd(alpha_r) = ( L_outer(alpha_r + h) - L_outer(alpha_r - h) ) / (2 h)\\n\\nThe stability proxy is:\\n\\n    rel_err_T2(method, r) = || g_method_r - g_ref_fd(alpha_r) ||_2 / || g_ref_fd(alpha_r) ||_2\\n\\nThe finite-difference reference cost is negligible compared to inner-solve cost at K >= 100 and is paid once per replicate per task; round-level timing is applied only to the method's own inner solve, not the reference.\\n\\nSecondary stability signals:\\n\\n    cond_Tx(method, r) = kappa(A_r)         # implicit-diff paths only\\n    outer_loss_var(method) = Var_r [ L_inner_terminal_r ]\\n\\nMemory proxy: `tracemalloc` peak around one inner solve per cell. Labelled explicitly as proxy, not primary.\\n\\nPer-cell artefacts recorded: `dt_r` series (length R), `g_method_r` series, `g_ref` series, `impl_residual_*` series (implicit-diff only), `cond_*` series (implicit-diff only), `L_inner_terminal_r` series, `tracemalloc` peak. All written in a single per-cell HDF5 / JSON artefact under (task, K, method, seed_master).\\n\\nThe exact TorchOpt API names used (e.g., for A, the function formerly or currently named `torchopt.diff.implicit.grad` [verify], and the solver argument list [verify]) are NOT locked until the Source-verification gate (ix) passes and the verified names are appended to the experiment report.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (vi) Statistical analysis\\n\\nUnit of analysis: one replicate `r in {1 .. R}` paired across methods by `seed_r` (and hence by `D_r` and initial-state seed).\\n\\nPrimary statistic. Wilcoxon signed-rank test, paired by `seed_r`, applied to:\\n- the timing vector `dt_r(method=baseline) - dt_r(method=Treatment)` for each (Treatment x baseline) pair;\\n- the stability vector `rel_err_r(method=baseline) - rel_err_r(method=Treatment)`, aligned in sign so that \\\"lower is better\\\" for the Treatment.\\n\\nMultiple-comparison correction. Holm correction across the family of comparisons within a (task, K) cell. Number of pairwise comparisons per cell equals Treatments x Baselines = 2 x 3 = 6 (Treatments A, B x Baselines C1, C2, C3) — under Holm this is the family controlling FWER at alpha = 0.05. Bonferroni is recorded as a sensitivity check (more conservative); Holm is the headline.\\n\\nReporting. Per cell:\\n- median and IQR (p25 / p75) of `dt_r` and of `rel_err_r`, separately for each method.\\n- p95 of `dt_r` as a tail-latency indicator.\\n- For each pairwise signed-rank test: the W statistic, Holm-adjusted p-value, and the median signed difference (Delta-tilde) with its 95% bootstrap CI (B = 2000 resamples).\\n- Pre-specified effect-size threshold: a meaningful difference is one where either (a) the Holm-adjusted p < 0.05 AND | Delta-tilde / max(p75 - p25 across the two methods, 1e-12) | >= 0.5, OR (b) the Holm-adjusted p >= 0.05, in which case the cell is reported as \\\"no detectable difference\\\" (NOT \\\"no effect\\\").\\n- Smaller-than-threshold effects are not claimed as wins or losses under this design.\\n\\nPower. With R = 30 paired replicates and alpha = 0.05 (two-sided), the Wilcoxon signed-rank test has approximately 80% power to detect effect sizes around 0.5 SD in the paired differences; this is the source of the | Delta-tilde / IQR | >= 0.5 pre-specified threshold above. 15%-level speed-ups are detectable in well-behaved cells; smaller speed-ups will be reported as \\\"no detectable difference\\\".\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (vii) Feasibility — what execution actually needs\\n\\nRuntime environment required (NOT available in the current host):\\n- Python >= 3.10 (the current host has 3.12.3, which is fine).\\n- `pip install torch torchopt numpy scipy` performed in a GPU-enabled environment with a CUDA build of PyTorch. The current host is bubblewrap with the network disabled and no PyTorch / TorchOpt preinstalled — it is not the right host for execution. A typical TorchOpt install path is `pip install torch torchopt`, with the GPU-CUDA build of torch targeted to the host CUDA driver.\\n- The Source-verification gate requires a TorchOpt source checkout (not just the wheel) so that the exact API names under import can be inspected; record the `git rev-parse HEAD`.\\n\\nCompute budget per cell (rough, to be calibrated at execution):\\n- T1 with K = 10, K = 100: a few seconds per replicate per method on one CPU core; with R = 30, one cell about a few minutes.\\n- T1 with K = 1000: tens of seconds per replicate per method; cell about tens of minutes.\\n- T2 with K = 10: seconds per replicate per method; T2 with K = 1000 dominates the budget at tens of minutes to hours per replicate. Cells under C2 (`create_graph=True`, full unroll) are the slowest.\\n- With methods x (T1, T2) x {10, 100, 1000} x R = 30 x 6 = 36 cells, total wall-clock is dominated by T2 x K = 1000 x C2 and the thread-sensitivity repeat (multiplier at most 2). Indicative order: low double-digit CPU-hours on a single pinned core, multiplies with any additional sensitivity sweeps.\\n\\nMinimal scope (priority 1, BUDGET-CONSTRAINED execution path): T1 only, K in {100, 1000}, all six methods, R = 30, no thread-sensitivity sweep, no tolerance sweep. Tests H1 and H2 along the convex-stability axis. Estimated wall-clock: low single-digit CPU-hours.\\n\\nAmbitious scope (full design as specified): T1 + T2 x K in {10, 100, 1000} x all six methods x R = 30 + thread-sensitivity (1 axis x 1 repeat) + tolerance sweep (3 values) x R = 30. Estimated wall-clock: low-to-mid double-digit CPU-hours.\\n\\nArtefacts. A version manifest; a Source-verification appendix; per-cell HDF5/JSON artefacts; a per-cell statistics table; an aggregate cross-cell table with Holm-adjusted p-values; an ablation table for the A vs C2 and B vs C2 comparisons.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (viii) Threats to validity\\n\\n- Environmental ceiling: CPU-only host. CUDA-leaning TorchOpt optimisations (kernel fusion, autograd-graph caching, compiled-graph reuse) are NOT in scope of measurement on this host. The time axis therefore reflects algorithmic-flop accounting and Python/CPU dispatch cost rather than end-to-end GPU performance. The stability axis is independent of GPU availability and remains a primary evidentiary signal here.\\n- BLAS dependency: the exact BLAS implementation (OpenBLAS vs MKL) is a co-variate with timing and, weakly, with numerical stability. Recorded in the manifest; not swept within the design because sweeping BLAS confounds the method comparison.\\n- Scheduler / thermal noise: `taskset -c 0-3`, `set_num_threads(1)`, `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1` mitigate but do not eliminate residual noise. Cells with median-to-p95 spread greater than 2x are reported with that caveat and not aggregated into headline comparisons without the thread-sensitivity check.\\n- Semantic drift of the TorchOpt API across versions: the Source-verification gate (ix) is the only mitigation. If the gate finds a non-matching surface, the design pivots; it does not relabel.\\n- Absence of a canonical CPU benchmark for TorchOpt: there is no published baseline of TorchOpt performance on CPU. This means the magnitude claims in H1 / H2 cannot be anchored against prior literature for this host; the experimental design generates fresh data without that anchor.\\n- Fairness of `create_graph=True` documented form: held by D4 to the standard form with no `allow_unused`, no `torch.compile`, no manual checkpointing. This may not represent practitioner best-practices; (x) Limitations notes the best-practices variant as a single-line future-path on a GPU host.\\n- Synthetic-data external validity: T1 and T2 use synthetic data so the conditioning axis is controllable, but the conclusions may not transfer to real datasets. Documented as a limitation rather than a threat-at-the-design-level.\\n- Single-task inner loop: a multi-task (MAML-style task averaging) inner loop is intentionally excluded (D1) to avoid confounding the reuse-claim test with task-averaging dynamics. Recorded here as a validity scope-limit, not a threat to the per-task conclusions.\\n- Scalar outer hyperparameter: the outer hyperparameter is scalar in both T1 and T2. Vector-valued outer hyperparameter would change the implicit-diff linear-system conditioning profile. Recorded here as a scope limit.\\n- Effect-size threshold calibration: the |Delta-tilde / IQR| >= 0.5 threshold is reasonable for R = 30 but is not a literature-standard for hypergradient comparisons; a more orthodox report adds paired Cohen's d and Cliff's delta, both of which are derivable from the recorded per-cell data without re-execution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (x) Limitations and open questions\\n\\n- Best-practices comparator, GPU path. As a single-line acknowledgement of D4: a `create_graph=True + torch.compile` baseline on a GPU host is the practitioner-best-practices comparator. It is not in this design's scope and is recorded only as the natural next step on a CUDA-equipped execution host.\\n- Multi-task inner loop. Whether the A / B / C2 separation of effects reproduces under task averaging (MAML-style) is open. The design's scope-limit is single-task; an extension would add a multi-task cell at K = 5 inner steps (matching canonical few-shot setup) on a single representative task distribution, but only as an additional experiment.\\n- Vector outer hyperparameter. Open question: does the implicit-diff reuse advantage persist (or grow) when the outer hyperparameter is vector-valued (multiple regularizer strengths or per-layer learning rates)? The current design restricts the outer hyperparameter to scalar in both tasks to keep the analytical reference for T1 and the FD reference for T2 tractable.\\n- K range extrapolation. The K-sweep {10, 100, 1000} spans the MAML-relevant inner-loop range; extrapolation beyond K = 1000 is unverified.\\n- Source drift rebuild cadence. The Source-verification gate runs once per execution; if TorchOpt revises the implicit-diff API between the gate and the run, the design should re-gate.\\n- Hypergradient reference for T2. Central finite differences at h approx 1e-5 in float64 are the chosen reference for T2; Richardson extrapolation is permissible as a sensitivity check but not as the primary reference. The O(h^2) truncation error of central differences is a small known bias in `g_ref_fd` that translates to a known floor in `rel_err_T2`. Cells whose `rel_err_T2` is comparable to or below this floor are reported as \\\"below FD precision floor\\\" rather than as \\\"no error\\\".\\n- Outer-loss variance. Reported as a secondary stability signal across seeds; not a primary axis.\\n- Memory proxy specificity. `tracemalloc` is a Python-allocation-level proxy, not a tensor-level memory profile. On a CPU host this is the best available proxy short of intercepting PyTorch's allocator; it is reported as such.\\n- No execution in this environment. Per the environment ceiling and D5: this is a design-only artefact. No experiment was run; no per-cell artefacts were produced; no plots are claimed.\\n\\n— end of design —\",\"evidence\":\"Designer-recorded facts (no claim is made beyond what these ground):\\n- Repository paths and contents: /workspace/task.json is the only file in /workspace; find /workspace -maxdepth 3 returns only that file.\\n- TorchOpt presence on the host: find / -iname '*torchopt*' returns zero results; no TorchOpt source or wheel is anywhere on the filesystem.\\n- Python and module status: python3 -V -> 3.12.3; import torch -> ModuleNotFoundError; import torchopt -> ModuleNotFoundError; import numpy -> ModuleNotFoundError. No project dependency is installed.\\n- task.json self-declares Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\n- Environment summary from the wrapper context: bubblewrap runtime, network disabled, gpu_enabled=false, cpu_affinity_count=24.\\n- track.md is referenced inside task.json's documents field but does not exist on disk.\\n- No TorchOpt API names, function signatures, default tolerance values, or quoted paper claims were taken from local source; every TorchOpt-named call in pseudo-example code carries an explicit [verify] marker indicating that the name is documented-intention only and must be confirmed against an installed source checkout before execution.\\n\\nBinding decisions applied verbatim from the researcher review:\\n- D1 scope: T1 (ridge) + T2 (2-layer MLP); T3 (CNN) dropped by default, included only if the stability axis becomes underdetermined during writing; this did not occur, so T3 is not written into the design.\\n- D2 API surface: retained candidates with a Source-verification gate placed before section (iii) covering both (a) unavailability and (b) the rewrite-not-relabel rule; pseudo-example code carries [verify] markers.\\n- D3 environment: float64 only, CPU only, torch.cuda.Event unavailable recorded, omp/mkl_num_threads=1 + set_num_threads(1) primary, set_num_threads(4) sensitivity probe, taskset -c 0-3, software-version manifest required.\\n- D4 fairness for create_graph=True: standard documented form; best-practices variant with torch.compile is a single-line acknowledgement in (x) for a GPU host.\\n- D5 metrics hierarchy: both primary in general; on this CPU host stability + algorithmic flops is primary and time is secondary with an explicit preamble that CUDA TorchOpt optimisations are out of scope.\\n- D6 reuse-claim isolation: A vs B vs C2 is the primary 3-way ablation; with a fallback simplification to two independent (A vs C2) and (B vs C2) comparisons if the source-verification gate finds TorchOpt does not expose two distinct paths.\\n\\nNo execution was performed; no results, no pseudocode-output, no projected plot was claimed anywhere in this design.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"R = 30 paired replicates is the minimum execution budget; no claim of higher statistical power is made; smaller-than-threshold effects will not be reported as wins or losses.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"Pre-specified effect-size threshold: |Delta-tilde / max(IQR, 1e-12)| >= 0.5 with Holm-adjusted p < 0.05 is the bar for meaningful difference; non-significant cells are reported as no detectable difference.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"H1 and H2 are stated as a-priori directional expectations only; no specific magnitude, factor, or numerical target is claimed anywhere in the design.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"D6 binding: the design presents A vs B vs C2 as a 3-way ablation; if the source-verification gate finds TorchOpt exposes only one differentiable path, the design simplifies to (A vs C2) and (B vs C2) as two independent comparisons without other changes.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"D5 binding: both metrics are primary in general; on this CPU host stability plus algorithmic-flop accounting is the primary evidentiary axis and time is secondary, with the explicit acknowledgement that CUDA TorchOpt optimisations are out of scope.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"D4 binding: create_graph=True baseline uses the standard documented form without allow_unused, torch.compile, or manual checkpointing; the best-practices variant is acknowledged only in section (x) as a future GPU-host path.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"D3 binding: float64 throughout; CPU only; torch.cuda.Event is unavailable; threads pinned to 1 in the primary run with a single 4-thread sensitivity repeat; CPU affinity pinned to cores 0-3; software manifest is a required artefact.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"D2 binding: every TorchOpt invocation in pseudo-example code is marked [verify]; the design does not proceed past the Source-verification gate unless API names are confirmed against an actual source checkout.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"D1 binding: design executes T1 (ridge) + T2 (2-layer MLP) only; T3 (CNN) is not present and is recorded as drop-default per the researcher decision.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"The traceback signal is the API surface mapping draws on public TorchOpt documentation at large and is not confirmed against an installed source in this environment; this is the explicit reason for the Source-verification gate in section (ix) and for [verify] markers on every TorchOpt call.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Whether the stability axis would have remained underdetermined if T3 had been included in the design; the conditional was recorded but, per D1, did not trigger during writing.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"What the implicit-system matrix condition number kappa(A) actually looks like at K in {100, 1000} for T1 and T2; no claim is made in absence of data.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the |Delta-tilde / IQR| >= 0.5 effect-size bar is the right threshold for hypergradient comparisons; alternative thresholds (e.g., paired Cohen's d) may classify the same cells differently.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether OMP/MKL_NUM_THREADS=1 + set_num_threads(1) + taskset -c 0-3 is sufficient to suppress scheduler noise to the level required for roughly 15% effect detection.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the finite-difference reference on T2 has a precision floor that competes with the rel_err observations; cells near the FD precision floor are reported explicitly as such.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the synthetic T1 / T2 datasets are representative enough to generalise to real-data workloads.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the scalar-outer restriction in T1 and T2 limits external validity to scalar hyper-parameter problems; vector-valued outer hyperparameters are out of scope.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the K-sweep {10, 100, 1000} is sufficient to characterise the asymptotic scaling difference between Treatments and C2; extrapolation beyond K = 1000 is unverified by this design.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the TorchOpt version available at execution exposes two distinct differentiable paths (Treatment A implicit-diff reuse and Treatment B MetaOptimizer-state reuse) or only one; the design pivots but does not relabel per section (ix)(b).</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-479e8627-71cf-45ea-a011-80f243193b72", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 46}
